INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1007/s41365-026-02003-3','Development of an adiabatic RF neutron spin flipper at the China Spallation Neutron Source','China Spallation Neutron Source에서 단열 RF 중성자 스핀 플리퍼 개발','Nuclear Science and Techniques','1001-8042','Xu Qin; Yu-Chen Dong; Wolfgang Kreuzpaintner; Xiao-Mian Li; Xin-Pei Meng; Matthew Musgrave; Long Tian; Xiao-Min Xiong; Fan Ye; Jun-Pei Zhang; Tian-Hao Wang; Xin Tong','2026-08-13','2026-08-13','2026-08-13','연구논문','원자로물리·해석','','','https://doi.org/10.1007/s41365-026-02003-3','https://doi.org/10.1007/s41365-026-02003-3',0,'2026-08-15','2026-08-16','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','CN','CN','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1007/s41365-026-02013-1','60Coγ-ray irradiation experiments and electrical modeling of through-silicon vias in three-dimensional integrated circuits','60Coγ-선 조사 실험 및 3차원 집적 회로에서 실리콘 관통 비아 전기 모델링','Nuclear Science and Techniques','1001-8042','Bo Liang; Jin-Hui Liu; Yu-Xiong Xue; Gang Liu; Quan Wang; Wu-Ying Ma','2026-08-13','2026-08-13','2026-08-13','연구논문','핵연료·재료','','','https://doi.org/10.1007/s41365-026-02013-1','https://doi.org/10.1007/s41365-026-02013-1',0,'2026-08-15','2026-08-16','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','CN','CN','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1007/s41365-026-02029-7','NDMKG: a multimodal knowledge graph for nuclear data','NDMKG: 핵 데이터에 대한 다중 방식 지식 그래프','Nuclear Science and Techniques','1001-8042','Yi-Qi Wei; Rui Shi; Fei-Xiang Zhao; Ling-Ling Wu; Xin-Hua Xu; Xian-Guo Tuo','2026-08-12','2026-08-12','2026-08-12','연구논문','기타 원자력','','','https://doi.org/10.1007/s41365-026-02029-7','https://doi.org/10.1007/s41365-026-02029-7',0,'2026-08-15','2026-08-16','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','CN','CN','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1007/s41365-026-02015-z','Measurement of high-resolution spectra for a laser-plasma accelerator-driven fast neutron source','레이저 플라즈마 가속기 구동 고속 중성자 소스를 위한 고해상도 스펙트럼 측정','Nuclear Science and Techniques','1001-8042','Hao Xu; Jie Feng; Bing-Zhan Shi; Ming-Yang Zhu; Ming-Xuan Wei; Xi-Chen Hu; Guo-Qiang Zhang; Xu-Lei Ge; Feng Liu; Bo-Yuan Li; Wen-Chao Yan; Xiao-Hui Yuan; Li-Ming Chen','2026-08-12','2026-08-12','2026-08-12','연구논문','원자로물리·해석','','','https://doi.org/10.1007/s41365-026-02015-z','https://doi.org/10.1007/s41365-026-02015-z',0,'2026-08-15','2026-08-16','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','CN','CN','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1007/s41365-026-02012-2','Development of a strip LGAD sensor with double-end readout for future colliders','향후 콜라이더를 위한 더블 엔드 판독이 가능한 스트립 LGAD 센서 개발','Nuclear Science and Techniques','1001-8042','Wei-Yi Sun; Meng-Zhao Li; Tian-Yuan Zhang; Mei Zhao; Yun-Yun Fan; Shu-Qi Li; Yuan Feng; Xin-Hui Huang; Xuan Yang; Wei Wang; Zhi-Jun Liang; Yue-Kun Heng','2026-08-12','2026-08-12','2026-08-12','연구논문','기타 원자력','','','https://doi.org/10.1007/s41365-026-02012-2','https://doi.org/10.1007/s41365-026-02012-2',0,'2026-08-15','2026-08-16','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','CN','CN','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1007/s41365-026-02031-z','Machine learning methods for event classification and vertex reconstruction in the 12C + 12C reaction with the MATE-TPC','MATE-TPC와의 12C + 12C 반응에서 이벤트 분류 및 정점 재구성을 위한 기계 학습 방법','Nuclear Science and Techniques','1001-8042','Ming-Hui Zhang; Xiao-Bin Li; Jie Chen; Ning-Tao Zhang; Fen-Hua Lu; Jun-Rui Ma; Jia-Zhen Yan; Wan-Qin Tu; Xiao-Dong Tang; Bing-Shui Gao; Chen-Gui Lu; Zhi-Chao Zhang; Jin-Long Zhang; Wei-Ping Liu','2026-08-18','2026-08-18','2026-08-18','연구논문','AI·디지털','','','https://doi.org/10.1007/s41365-026-02031-z','https://doi.org/10.1007/s41365-026-02031-z',1,'2026-08-29','2026-08-30','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','CN','CN','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1007/s41365-026-02036-8','Modeling the instrumental response in CSNS neutron total scattering experiments involving inelastic samples','비탄성 샘플을 포함하는 CSNs 중성자 총 산란 실험에서 계측 응답 모델링','Nuclear Science and Techniques','1001-8042','Ni Yang; Zi-Yi Pan; Ming Tang; Wen Yin; Xiao-Xiao Cai','2026-09-05','2026-09-05','2026-09-05','연구논문','원자로물리·해석','','','https://doi.org/10.1007/s41365-026-02036-8','https://doi.org/10.1007/s41365-026-02036-8',1,'2026-09-06','2026-09-07','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','CN','CN','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106612','Nonlinear behavior of GTR for PWR fuel rods: Theoretical modeling, surrogate-based approach, and experimental comparison','PWR 핵연료봉에 대한 GTR의 비선형 거동: 이론적 모델링, 대리 접근 및 실험적 비교','Progress in Nuclear Energy','0149-1970','Jinming Li; Peng Li; Heng Huang; Guoyan Huang; Shuo Ma','2026-09-26','','2027-01','연구논문','핵연료·재료','In pressurized water reactor (PWR) fuel assemblies, fuel rods are restrained and supported through the grid-to-rod (GTR) system. During large-amplitude vibration, fuel-rod rotation changes not only the deformation of the grid spring but also the direction of the contact load acting on the pre-compressed post-buckled spring leaf. However, this rotation-induced variation of the spring loading direction is not explicitly represented in many existing engineering-oriented GTR models, in which the spring constraint is commonly described by linear, piecewise-linear, or prescribed nonlinear relations. To address this limitation, the present study extends our previous large-deformation formulation for a normally loaded post-buckled spring leaf to inclined-loading conditions. Based on post-buckling beam theory and the principle of virtual work, a nonlinear mechanical relationship among spring deformation, loading direction, and contact force is established, thereby enabling the spring force to evolve with both fuel-rod displacement and rotation. The proposed formulation is assessed through quasi-static experiments under axial pre-compression, normal loading, and inclined loading, showing that the predicted force–deformation characteristics agree well with the experimental observations within the investigated range. The mechanically derived spring relation is then incorporated into an established GTR contact framework, where fuel-rod displacement, rotation, and dimple lift-off/re-contact jointly determine the nonlinear restoring force and moment. Dynamic analyses show that the effect of the varying spring loading direction becomes increasingly pronounced as the vibration amplitude and rod rotation increase, producing response characteristics that cannot be captured by a fixed-direction spring representation. To enable efficient repeated evaluation of the nonlinear spring relation during time integration, a neural-network surrogate is further employed as a computational approximation of the direct mechanical solution. The present work therefore provides a mechanics-based extension of GTR spring modeling from fixed-direction loading to rotation-dependent inclined loading, and establishes a basis for incorporating this mechanism into more comprehensive fuel-rod and fuel-assembly dynamic models.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106612','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106612',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','CN','CN','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.net.2026.104743','Thermal study of air conditioning systems for temporary spent nuclear fuel dry storage facilities','한시적 사용후핵연료 건식저장시설 냉난방 시스템에 대한 열적 연구','Nuclear Engineering and Technology','1738-5733','Wen-Yu Wang; Bo-Shuan You; Ming-You Weng','2026-09-26','','2026-09','연구논문','열수력·유체','Dry storage is a key technology of spent nuclear fuel (SNF) management. However, no previous studies have examined the application of air-conditioning (AC) systems to indoor dry storage facilities. This study investigates the cooling strategies to reduce temperatures in an indoor temporary SNF dry storage facility during the decommissioning stage of the Chinshan Nuclear Power Plant in Taiwan. A three-dimensional steady-state CFD model was developed in ANSYS Fluent to analyze the indoor temperature field, airflow characteristics, and heat removal performance. The effects of passive natural convection and active AC systems were investigated. The results show that the passive ventilation design provides only limited heat-removal capability because the indoor airflow is mainly governed by buoyancy-driven natural convection and is considerably constrained by the cask arrangement. Adjusting the number or size of windows can slightly modify the local airflow path, but overall cooling improvement remains limited. Meanwhile, an AC system effectively enhances air exchange and reduces indoor heat accumulation. The results show that installing two AC units is sufficient to reduce temperatures to below 33°C at a height of 2 m. This study provides practical design guidance to improve the thermal safety and working environment in indoor SNF dry storage facilities.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.net.2026.104743','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.net.2026.104743',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','TW','TW','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.net.2026.104733','Physical design and performance optimization of high-granularity detector arrays for the first μSR spectrometer at CSNS','CSN에서 첫 번째 μSR 분광기를 위한 고입도 검출기 어레이의 물리적 설계 및 성능 최적화','Nuclear Engineering and Technology','1738-5733','Tianyi Yang; Ziwen Pan; Qi Yuan; Qiang Li; Yu Bao; Hao Liang; Bangjiao Ye','2026-09-26','','2026-09','연구논문','원자로물리·해석','The Phase II upgrade project of the China Spallation Neutron Source (CSNS-II) includes the construction of a surface muon beamline and a muon spin rotation/relaxation/resonance ( μ SR) spectrometer, which will be the first such instrument built in China. Taking into magnet design, sample environment, and sample chamber constraints, we have developed three conceptual detector array configurations: a dual spherical-shell structure, a cylindrical structure, and a single spherical-shell structure. Each configuration comprises 2782 to 3024 detector units to maximize counting rate. Simulations under zero-field and transverse-field conditions were conducted using the Geant4 toolkit to evaluate detector uniformity and overall spectrometer performance, including asymmetry, counting rate, and figure of merit. These results provide critical parameters and insights for the design and construction of the first μ SR spectrometer detector system at CSNS.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.net.2026.104733','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.net.2026.104733',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','CN','CN','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.nucengdes.2026.115220','Numerical investigation of ex-vessel sodium ejection under energetic CDA in a pool-type SFR','수영장형 SFR에서 정력적 CDA 하의 전 혈관 소듐 배출에 대한 수치 조사','Nuclear Engineering and Design','0029-5493','B. Thilak; P. Mangarjuna Rao','2026-09-26','','2026-12','연구논문','기타 원자력','','','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nucengdes.2026.115220','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nucengdes.2026.115220',0,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','','','OpenAlex: country unavailable');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.nucengdes.2026.115232','Identification and ranking on thermal hydraulic phenomena in integrated SMR during station blackout','스테이션 정전 중 통합 SMR에서 열수력 현상에 대한 식별 및 순위','Nuclear Engineering and Design','0029-5493','YuSheng Liu; Chao Xu; XiaoJun Qiu; LeHan Sun; JianPing Jing; SiChao Tan','2026-09-26','','2026-12','연구논문','열수력·유체','The Phenomena Identification and Ranking Table (PIRT), as a systematic tool in nuclear safety analysis, is widely used to identify key physical phenomena in accident scenarios and assess their importance and level of knowledge. Although the traditional PIRT method has the advantage of structured analysis, its strong reliance on experts'' subjective judgments affects the objectivity and repeatability of ranking process. This study mainly focuses on thermal-hydraulic (T-H) phenomena occurring in a typical small modular pressurized water reactor (SMPWR) during station blackout (SBO) accident, and proposes a phenomena importance ranking method integrating response surface analysis (RSA). The one dimensional numerical simulation node model is established to simulate the entire SBO accident process, and to represent and identify the T-H phenomena. The multi-parameter RSA is conducted to quantify the impact of various T-H phenomena on two main figures of merit, namely the pressure of reactor coolant system (RCS) and the liquid level of core. The SBO accident is divided into three phases, and key phenomena in important systems and components such as reactor pressure vessel (RPV), core, once-through steam generator (OTSG), and passive safety systems are systematically identified. The results obtained by RSA indicate that phenomena such as tube-side inventory variation of OTSG, natural circulation (NC), and stea m -liquid interaction have significant impacts on accident progress. Importantly, the tube-side parameters of OTSG continuously influence the core level and RCS pressure through the decay heat removal system (DHRS) even in the long-term cooling phase mainly dominated by emergency core cooling system (ECCS). The tube-side parameters of OTSG play prominent role in safety of SMPWRs. Compared with traditional PIRT method that solely relies on expert judgment, the PIRT in combination with RSA significantly enhances the objectivity and reliability of T-H phenomena ranking. Not only a detailed SBO PIRT for SMPWR is proposed in this investigation but also an important basis for design optimization and experimental verification are provided. This approach can be used to further promote the scientificity and objectivity of the PIRT development process in nuclear safety analysis for small modular reactors (SMRs).','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nucengdes.2026.115232','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nucengdes.2026.115232',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','CN','CN','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.nucengdes.2026.115214','Experimental investigation of surface inclination effects on ablation of a solid impacted by a hot liquid jet','뜨거운 액체 제트에 의해 충격을 받은 고체의 절제에 대한 표면 경사 효과의 실험적 조사','Nuclear Engineering and Design','0029-5493','Asma Djermoune; Alexandre Lecoanet; Nathalie Seiler; Nicolas Rimbert; Michel Gradeck','2026-09-26','','2026-12','연구논문','열수력·유체','In sodium-cooled fast reactors (SFRs), the potential consequences of a severe accident with core meltdown are now considered during the reactor design phase. Such events lead to the formation of a corium , a radioactive mixture of molten nuclear fuel and structural materials. Two protective devices can be implemented in advanced SFR designs: discharge tubes, used to transfer the corium to the lower part of the reactor vessel to prevent recriticality, and a core catcher, designed to receive the corium to facilitate its long-term cooling. However, with such new mitigation designs, the corium may impinge on the core catcher as a high-temperature liquid jet ( ∼ 3000 K ), which can lead to localized thermal ablation of the core catcher. Some design proposals for the core catcher include angled baffles intended to dissipate the jet’s kinetic energy. The ablation of such inclined surfaces must be understood to ensure the structural integrity of the core catcher during the corium relocation phase. The present study investigates the influence of surface inclination on the ablation dynamics induced by a hot free-surface liquid jet impinging on an inclined solid target. Experiments were conducted using the HAnSoLO facility, in which water and ice serve as simulant materials, with a water jet impinging on a transparent ice block. The experimental conditions cover Reynolds numbers from 3.7 × 1 0 4 to 1.47 × 1 0 5 , Prandtl numbers from 2.5 to 5.4, and surface inclination angles from 0° to 45°. Results show that surface inclination strongly affects the filling of the cavity as well as the flow circulation before the pool regime. A new intermediate regime, absent for horizontal impacts, was identified. The results show that surface inclination is not necessarily beneficial in terms of ablation reduction and that, at the highest Reynolds numbers investigated, inclination can lead to an increase in the ablation velocity. These findings provide a first experimental basis for understanding the influence of surface inclination on jet-induced ablation and for guiding further investigations toward reactor-relevant conditions. Their extension to corium conditions will require a progressive validation approach combining numerical simulations and prototypical experiments.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nucengdes.2026.115214','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nucengdes.2026.115214',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','FR','FR','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.nucengdes.2026.115225','A numerical method for coupling parameterized physics-informed neural networks and FDM for advanced thermal-hydraulic system simulation','매개변수화된 물리 정보 신경망과 고급 열수력 시스템 시뮬레이션을 위한 FDM을 결합하기 위한 수치 방법','Nuclear Engineering and Design','0029-5493','Jeesuk Shin; Donggyun Seo; Sihyeong Yu; Joongoo Jeon','2026-09-26','','2026-12','연구논문','열수력·유체','Severe accident analysis with system codes such as MELCOR is indispensable for nuclear safety assessment, yet the cost of repeated simulations constrains parametric studies and uncertainty quantification. Data-driven surrogates accelerate these analyses but require large simulation datasets, whereas physics-informed neural networks (PINNs) require no training data yet must be retrained for every parameter change and lose accuracy over long horizons. This study addresses both limitations with the P2F method — Parameterized PINNs coupled with the finite difference method (FDM) — for MELCOR’s Control Volume Hydrodynamics/Flow Path (CVH/FL) module. A parameterized Node-Assigned PINN (NA-PINN) takes the water-level difference, initial velocity, and time as inputs, learning a solution manifold so that a single trained network serves as a data-free surrogate for the momentum equation at all flow paths. The parameterized NA-PINN is coupled with an FDM solver that advances the mass conservation equation at each time step, conserving mass exactly in the discrete sense while replacing the iterative nonlinear momentum solution with a single forward pass. Because the network is evaluated only within one time step and re-anchored to the FDM-updated state, the long-horizon training limitation is avoided. For a six-tank gravity-driven draining scenario, the water-level mean absolute error was 9 . 3 2 × 1 ⁢ 0 − 5 m and the velocity error 3 . 0 8 × 1 ⁢ 0 − 3 m/s at 𝛥 ⁢ 𝑡 = 1 . 0 s. This accuracy holds for time steps from 0.2 to 1.0 s and five initial conditions without retraining or data. To the best of the authors’ knowledge, this is the first data-free parameterized PINN coupled with an FDM solver for thermal-hydraulic system-code modeling.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nucengdes.2026.115225','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nucengdes.2026.115225',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','KR','KR','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112863','Stochastic uncertainty quantification and model validation for burnup calculations in heat pipe-cooled reactors','열파이프 냉각 원자로에서 연소 계산을 위한 확률적 불확실성 정량화 및 모델 검증','Annals of Nuclear Energy','0306-4549','Jianshu Qiao; Dongli Huang; Tao Zhou; Youyuan Fu; Jun Wu','2026-09-26','','2027-02','연구논문','열수력·유체','Burnup calculations play a critical role in predicting the neutronic behavior and fuel evolution of nuclear reactors over their operating cycle. However, uncertainties in nuclear data propagated through these calculations can lead to significant deviations in key reactor responses. This challenge is particularly acute for long-lifetime non-refuelable systems, such as heat pipe-cooled reactors (HPRs), where accumulated uncertainties cannot be mitigated via fuel management strategies. Such uncertainties are typically addressed with conservative treatments, which reduce confidence in model predictions and introduce excessive safety margins. In this study, a stochastic uncertainty quantification framework is developed to investigate the propagation of nuclear data uncertainties in HPR burnup calculations. Uncertainty propagation is executed through Monte Carlo-based stochastic depletion simulations. To constrain predictive uncertainty, a model validation approach based on a Copula dependence model is introduced, leveraging measurable isotopic concentrations. Results indicate that the uncertainty in k eff increases progressively along the burnup chain due to cumulative effects. Specifically, fissile isotopes such as U-235 exhibit rising uncertainties with burnup, while plutonium isotopes show distinct neutron capture production mechanisms. Furthermore, power distribution analysis reveals elevated uncertainty levels near the core boundary, attributed to neutron leakage. Incorporation of isotopic concentration constraints reduces the uncertainty in k eff by approximately 30%, offering a robust pathway to reduce unnecessary design margins.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112863','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112863',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','BD;CN','BD','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112837','SKCSP-YOLOv5: a multi-scale feature enhancement network with attention for bubble detection in gas–liquid two-phase flow','SKCSP-YOLOv5: 기체-액체 2-상 흐름에서 기포 검출에 주의를 기울이는 다중 스케일 기능 향상 네트워크','Annals of Nuclear Energy','0306-4549','Asim Ullah Muhammad; Shen Jihong; Wang Shujuan; Qiao Shouxu; Awais Khan; Yaqoob Raffay','2026-09-26','','2027-02','연구논문','열수력·유체','Accurate bubble detection in rod bundle two-phase flow is essential for nuclear reactor safety, underpinning void fraction estimation and thermal–hydraulic model validation, yet existing YOLO-based detectors struggle to jointly resolve small, densely packed, and deformable bubbles. This paper proposes SKCSP-YOLOv5, which integrates Selective Kernel Cross-Stage-Partial (SKCSP) blocks, a Convolutional Block Attention Module (CBAM), and an additional P6/64 detection head that provides a coarse-scale prediction level for the largest individual bubbles, while retaining the same single-bubble detection target across all four detection heads. Across five independent training seeds, SKCSP-YOLOv5 achieves a mean recall of 86.5% and mAP@0.5 of 91.0%, significantly outperforming parameter-matched YOLOv5m, YOLOv8m, and YOLOv12m baselines (19.6–25.9 M parameters; p 6) while requiring 25.4 GFLOPs, roughly a third to a half the computational cost of these baselines. Performance is further characterized by bubble size, local density, and occlusion level, together with counting and sizing error, to assess suitability for quantitative bubble characterization beyond aggregate detection accuracy. These results establish SKCSP-YOLOv5 as an accurate, computationally efficient, and statistically validated foundation for automated bubble characterization in nuclear reactor subchannel flows.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112837','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112837',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','CN','CN','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112858','Noise-aware adaptive accelerated Picard coupling for stochastic OpenMC–OpenFOAM reactor multiphysics','확률적 OpenMC-OpenFOAM 원자로 다중물리을 위한 노이즈 인식 적응형 가속 피카드 커플링','Annals of Nuclear Energy','0306-4549','Han Yang; Ruixin Yang; Zhengkai Wang; Bingxin Ma; Jun Qin','2026-09-26','','2027-02','연구논문','열수력·유체','Monte Carlo (MC) neutronics–thermal-hydraulic partitioned coupling enables high-fidelity reactor multiphysics, but tally noise can destabilize classical Anderson acceleration under low-particle conditions. This paper proposes Noise-Aware Adaptive Accelerated Picard (NAAP), an outer-iteration algorithm that uses MC tally relative standard deviation as a coupling-control signal. NAAP combines noise-aware weighted least squares, noise-gated history rejection, and noise-dependent damping with an adaptive three-stage switch among under-relaxed Picard iteration, noise-aware Anderson acceleration, and residual-balance early stopping, together with a dual convergence criterion. In a reflected 17 × 17 assembly-level PWR real-coupling numerical study, standard Anderson acceleration with m = 5 produced a 35% residual-jump failure rate in a 20-seed ensemble. NAAP ref5 had 0% failures, entered Stage 2 in 100% of the runs, and reduced the median final residual from 9.213 × 1 0 − 4 for Picard to 5.058 × 1 0 − 4 . In an extended convergence study, NAAP ref5 reached the practical residual threshold of 5 × 1 0 − 4 in 5/5 runs with a median of 6 outer iterations, whereas Picard reached this threshold in 0/5 runs within 30 iterations. In reduced real-coupling auxiliary LFR and SFR numerical studies, standard Anderson triggered residual-jump failures in 4/5 and 2/5 seeds, respectively, while NAAP default and NAAP ref5 triggered no failures. These results indicate that Anderson residual jumps are not PWR-specific and that the NAAP safeguard remains effective under fast-spectrum liquid-metal conditions. Because NAAP acts only at the coupling layer and does not modify OpenMC or OpenFOAM, it provides an interpretable stability-preserving acceleration safeguard for MC-thermal-hydraulic partitioned coupling.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112858','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112858',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','CN','CN','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112874','Development of a CPU/GPU heterogeneous code for dynamic gamma dose field calculation in nuclear decommissioning operations','원자력 해체 작업에서 동적 감마선량 필드 계산을 위한 CPU/GPU 이기종 코드 개발','Annals of Nuclear Energy','0306-4549','Jifeng Hu; Yongkuo Liu; Zhitao Chen; Nan Chao; Xiaochang Zheng; Zhongkun Liu; Pengshuai Wang','2026-09-26','','2027-02','연구논문','핵연료주기·폐기물','','','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112874','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112874',0,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','','','OpenAlex: country unavailable');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112854','A deep learning-based surrogate model for Severe Accidents in nuclear reactors using ASTEC','ASTEC을 사용한 원자로의 심각한 사고에 대한 딥 러닝 기반 대용 모델','Annals of Nuclear Energy','0306-4549','Alessandro Longhi; Danny Lathouwers; Zoltán Perkó','2026-09-26','','2027-02','연구논문','열수력·유체','Integral codes like the Accident Source Term Evaluation Code (ASTEC) are powerful tools to study the physics of Severe Accidents (SAs) in nuclear reactors. Real time SA simulators can also be helpful in training operators of nuclear plants to react correctly to malfunctions. However, SA simulators can take up to several days per simulation, making their use infeasible for real time applications. In this work we show how to speed up a SA simulator with a fast, Deep Learning based (DL), surrogate model (SM). The SM is built as a combination of a dimensionality reduction stage, via an AutoEncoder, and a time-stepping stage, via a Neural Ordinary Differential Equation. The data on which the SM is trained are obtained from the ASTEC simulator, by sampling a set of operator actions for station blackout (SBO) and loss-of-coolant accidents (LOCA). The objective of the developed SM is to approximate multiple spatio-temporal fields for the thermal-hydraulic physics, core degradation, and fission product release modules in ASTEC''s vessel domain. The SM predicts simultaneously around $80$ different physical variables (both scalar and fields), maintaining a stable autoregressive rollout up to $50$ thousand time steps. In addition, the AutoEncoder achieves a dimensionality reduction by a factor of over $300$, which allows the SM to predict up to $40$ hours of simulation in under a minute, both on CPU and GPU. This work is the first study of the capabilities and limits of DL based surrogate modeling in approximating the challenging, highly non-linear physics of ASTEC.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112854','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112854',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','','','OpenAlex: country unavailable');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106614','Sintering behavior and microstructural evolution of UO2-Gd2O3 fuel processed by spark plasma sintering','스파크 플라즈마 소결에 의해 처리된 UO2-Gd2O3 연료의 소결 거동 및 미세 구조적 진화','Progress in Nuclear Energy','0149-1970','Artur Cesar de Freitas; Diogo Ribeiro Costa; Elita Fontenele Urano de Carvalho; Ricardo Mendes Leal Neto; Pär Olsson; Michelangelo Durazzo','2026-09-25','','2027-01','연구논문','핵연료·재료','This study systematically examines the sintering behavior of pure UO 2 and UO 2 pellets containing 10 wt% Gd 2 O 3 processed via conventional sintering and Spark Plasma Sintering (SPS). The analysis focuses on densification mechanisms, microstructural evolution, and diffusion-driven features related to local Gd redistribution. The results indicate that, under conventional sintering conditions, the incorporation of Gd 2 O 3 promotes the development of Kirkendall-type porosity due to the disparity in diffusion rates between Gd 3+ and U 4+ cations. This effect leads to increased residual porosity and a consequent reduction in densification of the doped pellets. In contrast, SPS processing yields significantly enhanced densification, with relative densities exceeding 97% of the theoretical density, even in the presence of Gd 2 O 3 . The corresponding microstructures are characterized by a higher degree of compaction and a predominance of fine, isolated pores, indicative of improved sintering efficiency. Energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) mapping reveals a heterogeneous spatial distribution of gadolinium in samples processed by both routes, reflecting limitations in powder mixing and diffusion homogenization. Although forced powder mixing contributes to improved microstructural uniformity, its effect on densification remains comparatively limited. Overall, the findings highlight the superior capability of SPS to mitigate diffusion-induced porosity and to promote enhanced densification and microstructural refinement in UO 2 -Gd 2 O 3 systems.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106614','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106614',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','BR;SE','BR','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106626','Coupled neutronics–thermal–thermal expansion analysis of a heat pipe-cooled reactor using the SARAX code','SARAX 코드를 사용한 히트 파이프 냉각 원자로의 커플링된 중성자-열-열 팽창 분석','Progress in Nuclear Energy','0149-1970','Haoxiang Xu; Youqi Zheng; Xianan Du; Jintao Guo; Yu Han; Geng Zhang','2026-09-25','','2027-01','연구논문','열수력·유체','Heat pipe cooled reactors are perceived as promising for providing power supply in remote grids owing to their compactness and safety properties. In this context, thermal expansion plays an important role in inducing sufficient negative reactivity during operation and accident conditions, and thus must be considered in neutronics analysis of reactors of this type. In this paper, a coupled neutronics–thermal–thermal expansion calculation framework is developed within the core calculation module of the SARAX code for heat pipe reactor analysis, with the Megapower reactor as a case study. The coupled framework consists of three main solvers, as follows. The neutronics solver solves the neutron transport equation on arbitrary triangular prism geometry, providing the neutron flux and power distribution. The thermal solver, which includes heat pipe simulation, solves heat conduction in hexagonal geometry and provides temperature distributions for both hexagonal and triangular prism regions. The thermal expansion solver computes axial and radial expansion ratios, repositions mesh points, and updates the geometry accordingly. The axial expansion is assumed to be uniform within each layer, whereas radial expansion is represented by the non-uniform repositioning of mesh points, with their positions determined using the spring method. The three solvers are coupled through a Picard iteration scheme on a unified mesh system. Based on the coupled solution, the reactivity feedback from the temperature distribution is calculated to be −714 pcm, while that from thermal expansion is −640 pcm, yielding a total negative reactivity of −1354 pcm. The temperature distributions of the fuel and matrix were relatively uniform inside the core region, with only a minor impact on the power distribution. The maximum axial expansion reached 2 cm at the top, assuming the bottom layer remains fixed. The maximum radial shift of position in the fuel region was around 5.3 mm at the corners. These results demonstrate the effectiveness of the coupled method in capturing multiphysics feedback effects and thus provide a reliable tool for the neutronics analysis of heat pipe cooled reactors.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106626','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106626',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','CN','CN','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1080/00295450.2026.2735650','Enhancing Geiger-Müller Dosimetry Accuracy: Error Source Analysis and Process Improvement Using the 6 M Ishikawa Method','가이거 뮐러 선량 측정 정확도 향상: 6 M 이시카와 방법을 사용한 오류 소스 분석 및 프로세스 개선','Nuclear Technology','0029-5450','Ghada A. Khouqeer','2026-09-25','2026-09-25','2026-09-25','연구논문','안전·사고·리스크','Accurate dose measurement with Geiger-Müller (GM) dosimeters is essential for radiation protection and research, but these measurements are affected by numerous sources of uncertainty that can reduce the reliability of dose assessments. In this study, an Ishikawa diagram was applied to systematically identify and categorize the root causes of errors in GM dose measurements, addressing the need for a holistic understanding of uncertainty factors. The primary objective was to visually map all potential error sources and develop a quantitative uncertainty budget showing their relative contributions.The methodology involved constructing a cause-and-effect diagram that organized the error sources into six main categories: manpower (operator skills, training, positioning), machine (dead time, energy dependence, nonlinearity at high dose rates, detector aging), method (calibration protocol, background subtraction, correction procedures), material (gas composition, filter interactions, detector construction), mother nature (temperature, pressure, humidity, electromagnetic interference, background radiation), and measurement (counting statistics, scale resolution, field uniformity).An uncertainty budget, based on literature data and constructed in accordance with the Guide to the Expression of Uncertainty in Measurement (GUM), indicated a combined expanded uncertainty of approximately 54% at the 95% confidence level (k = 2). The main contributors were uncorrected dead-time losses (about 12.5%) and energy and angular dependence (about 23%). The structured 6 M analysis showed that combining the Ishikawa diagram with a quantitative GUM-compliant uncertainty budget provides both a diagnostic framework for identifying error sources and a prioritized hierarchy of corrective actions.This integration establishes a structured pathway from qualitative root-cause analysis to targeted mitigation strategies, enabling resources to be directed toward factors with the greatest effect on measurement accuracy. The findings emphasize the need for comprehensive uncertainty management in GM dosimetry to support reliable dose assessments, particularly in epidemiological and radiobiological studies where large uncertainties can obscure dose-response relationships.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1080/00295450.2026.2735650','https://doi.org/10.1080/00295450.2026.2735650',0,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','SA','SA','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.net.2026.104719','Response-dependent layer ordering in fixed-inventory compact neutron–photon shields: Exhaustive OpenMC benchmarks at 2.45 and 14.1 MeV','고정 인벤토리 콤팩트 중성자-광자 차폐물에서의 응답 의존적 레이어 정렬: 2.45 및 14.1 MeV에서의 철저한 OpenMC 벤치마크','Nuclear Engineering and Technology','1738-5733','Amani Kraishan','2026-09-25','','2026-09','연구논문','원자로물리·해석','Layer ordering can alter mixed-field shielding because each layer receives a neutron–photon field modified by upstream materials and leaves a different attenuation inventory downstream. This effect was isolated in a vacuum-bounded finite-slab shield containing 5 cm polyethylene, 5 cm borated polyethylene, 5 cm iron, and 10 cm concrete. All 24 permutations of this fixed inventory were evaluated with coupled neutron–photon OpenMC transport for monodirectional point-source neutrons at 2.45 and 14.1 MeV. At 2.45 MeV, neutron and photon detector responses varied by factors of 2.52 and 15.61, respectively; at 14.1 MeV, the factors were 1.94 and 1.79. The nominal neutron minimum changed from C17 (Fe–CONC–PE–BPE) to C23 (CONC–Fe–PE–BPE), while the photon minimum changed from C01 (PE–BPE–Fe–CONC) to C19 (CONC–PE–BPE–Fe). Conservative covariance-free leading tiers overlapped only partially. The Pareto sets shared only C13, C17, and C19 ( J P = 0.273 ). C13 remained the equal-weight minimizer under maximum and min–max normalization at both energies, whereas ratio-to-best normalization selected C01 at 2.45 MeV and C13 at 14.1 MeV. For the 2.45 MeV ICRP-116 analysis, C15 minimized the maximum-normalized equal-weight response in all six irradiation geometries. These results show that layer ordering is a distinct, response-dependent variable within the prescribed benchmarks, not a universal shielding rule.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.net.2026.104719','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.net.2026.104719',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','JO','JO','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.net.2026.104742','Radon gas increasing trend at Campi Flegrei caldera during 2011-2026','2011-2026년 동안 Campi Flegrei caldera에서 라돈 가스 증가 추세','Nuclear Engineering and Technology','1738-5733','Giuseppe La Verde; Marina Poje Sovilj; Vanja Radolić; Chiara Imparato; Carlo Sabbarese; Mariagabriella Pugliese; Fabrizio Ambrosino','2026-09-25','','2026-09','연구논문','기타 원자력','Campi Flegrei caldera is currently experiencing an ongoing phase of volcanic unrest characterized by increasing seismicity, ground uplift (bradyseism), and enhanced hydrothermal degassing. Continuous monitoring of geochemical parameters is therefore essential for improving the understanding of subsurface processes associated with caldera evolution. In this study, a unique 15-year continuous dataset of soil-gas 222 Rn activity concentrations, acquired by the RaMonA monitoring station between October 2011 and July 2026, was analysed together with local earthquake records and meteorological parameters. The influences of environmental variables on radon activity concentrations were evaluated using Spearman''s rank correlation and multiple linear regression. The results indicate that meteorological parameters have only a limited influence on the observed radon variability, with regression models explaining approximately 2.6% of the variance for soil parameters and 1.7% for atmospheric parameters. After accounting for these effects, the radon time series exhibits a persistent long-term increasing trend that temporally coincides with the progressive intensification of local seismicity, particularly during the recent phase of accelerated unrest from 2021 onwards. Although the observed relationship does not imply a direct predictive capability for individual earthquake, it suggests that radon variations respond to the long-term evolution of the hydrothermal and stress conditions within the caldera. The presented 15-year continuous monitoring record, unique in the literature, demonstrates a persistent long-term increase in soil-gas radon activity concentrations accompanying the progressive unrest of the Campi Flegrei caldera, highlighting radon as a valuable complementary geochemical parameter within integrated volcanic surveillance systems.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.net.2026.104742','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.net.2026.104742',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','HR;IT;TW','HR','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.net.2026.104725','Data-driven surrogate modeling of FLUKA-generated ambient dose equivalent distributions in an asymmetric proton accelerator shielding system','비대칭 양성자 가속기 차폐 시스템에서 FLUKA가 생성한 주변 선량 등가 분포의 데이터 기반 대리 모델링','Nuclear Engineering and Technology','1738-5733','Demet Sariyer','2026-09-25','','2027-02','연구논문','원자로물리·해석','Accurate determination of spatial ambient dose equivalent H*(10) distributions in high-energy proton accelerator facilities is essential for radiation protection and shielding design. In this study, spatial ambient dose equivalent H*(10) distributions produced under a proton beam-loss scenario were investigated using the FLUKA Monte Carlo (MC) code within an asymmetric multilayer concrete-ferroboron (FeB) shielding system. A 1000 MeV proton beam interacting with a copper target was modeled, and the resulting radiation field was analyzed in terms of secondary particle production, stopping-particle distributions, low-energy neutron interactions, neutron energy spectra, and spatial dose distributions. The results showed that neutrons are the dominant component of the secondary radiation field, accounting for 33.4% of all secondary particles. Protons represented 68.6% of the stopping-particle inventory, while neutrons constituted 96.0% of the particles generated by low-energy neutron interactions. Neutron spectrum analyses demonstrated that the concrete-FeB configuration significantly alters neutron transport through combined moderation and absorption mechanisms. The calculated H*(10) distributions exhibited strong directional dependence and pronounced dose gradients at material interfaces. Machine learning (ML)-based surrogate models, including Linear Regression (LR), K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN), Random Forest (RF), Extremely Randomized Trees (ETR), Gradient Boosting Regressor (GBR), Histogram-Based Gradient Boosting Regressor (HGBR), and Extreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost), were developed using FLUKA-generated data. Among them, ETR, RF, HGBR, and XGBoost achieved R 2 values exceeding 0.9818 under a randomized train-test split, accurately reproducing MC-generated dose distributions. However, blocked cross-validation revealed reduced predictive capability in previously unseen spatial regions. The trained models generated dose predictions within milliseconds, reducing computational time by several orders of magnitude compared with conventional MC simulations. These findings demonstrate the potential of the proposed hybrid MC-ML framework for rapid shielding assessment and optimization in proton accelerator facilities.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.net.2026.104725','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.net.2026.104725',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','TR','TR','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.net.2026.104740','Axial Development of Local Void-Fraction and Fluid-Temperature Distributions in Low-Pressure Subcooled Boiling in a Vertical Internally Heated Annulus','수직 내부 가열 환형에서 저압 아냉각 비등에서 국부 공극 분포 및 유체 온도 분포의 축 개발','Nuclear Engineering and Technology','1738-5733','Yeon-Gun Lee; Il-Woong Park; Hyeonjo Kim; Changyong Jung; Hyoungjin Na; Sin Kim','2026-09-25','','2026-09','연구논문','열수력·유체','ABSTRACTS The coupled evolution of interfacial structure and thermal fields in subcooled boiling remains difficult to predict, and reliable model validation requires local measurements of both phase distribution and temperature. In this study, an extended experimental database was established for low-pressure subcooled boiling flow in a vertically oriented internally heated annulus. Water-steam subcooled boiling flow was generated between a 30 mm-inner-diameter outer tube and a 10 mm-diameter heated rod over an effective heating length of 2.0 m. Six test conditions were examined by varying wall heat flux, mass flux, inlet subcooling, and exit pressure over ranges of 198–377 kW/m 2 , 376–680 kg/ m 2 s, 12.3–15.8 K, and 115–202 kPa, respectively. High-speed flow visualization was combined with previously measured local void fraction data obtained by optical fiber probes, together with measurements of radial fluid temperature profiles and heated surface temperatures at multiple axial locations. Based on these measurements, the coupled axial evolution of interfacial structure, fluid temperature distribution, and wall thermal response was systematically characterized under varying operating conditions. The resulting integrated dataset is expected to serve as a valuable benchmark for CFD-grade wall boiling closures and interfacial transport models for low-pressure subcooled boiling flows.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.net.2026.104740','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.net.2026.104740',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','KR','KR','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.nucengdes.2026.115224','Simulations of external flow around twisted elliptical tubes for above unity Prandtl numbers and low to moderate Reynolds numbers','위의 통일성 Prandtl 수와 낮은 Reynolds 수에서 중간 Reynolds 수에 대한 비틀린 타원형 튜브 주변의 외부 흐름 시뮬레이션','Nuclear Engineering and Design','0029-5493','Sierra A. Tutwiler; Dillon R. Shaver; Lane B. Carasik','2026-09-25','','2026-12','연구논문','열수력·유체','Twisted elliptical tubes are a proposed heat transfer enhancement (HTE) for use in Molten Salt Reactors (MSRs) due to their enhanced thermal performance compared to plain tubes. The twisting ellipsoid geometry causes the fluid to swirl as it passes over the tubes, enhancing mixing and inducing turbulence at lower Reynolds numbers. This effect increases overall convective heat transfer; however, this is at the cost of increased frictional pressure losses. Previous work by the authors has investigated the dependency of cross-sectional aspect ratio ( A R = 1.1 − 2.1 ) and below-unity Prandtl numbers ( P r = 0.001 − 1 ) for this geometry, and this study aims to extend this work by investigating above-unity Prandtl numbers and varying Reynolds numbers. In this study, the Nek5000 computational fluid dynamics (CFD) code is used to perform Large Eddy Simulations (LES) of external flow around twisted elliptical tube bundles. This study aims to further characterize the heat transfer phenomena seen around twisted elliptical tubes when varying the tube aspect ratio, which is a dependency that has not been historically accounted for. Simulations were performed for three different tube cross-sectional aspect ratios (maximum versus minimum diameter), Reynolds numbers of 2000 and 7000, and Prandtl numbers of 10, 20, and 30.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nucengdes.2026.115224','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nucengdes.2026.115224',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','US','US','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112868','Flow-induced vibration response of fuel rod based on transfer-matrix and modal-superposition methods with large-eddy simulation','대형 소용돌이 시뮬레이션을 사용한 이송 매트릭스 및 모달-중첩 방법에 기반한 연료 로드의 유동 유도 진동 응답','Annals of Nuclear Energy','0306-4549','Laishun Wang; Menghui Fang; Junyong Liu; Kelan Jian; Guangyun Min','2026-09-25','','2027-02','연구논문','열수력·유체','Flow-induced vibration (FIV) of fuel rods in axial coolant flow can drive grid-to-rod fretting and, in severe cases, lead to cladding failure, motivating high-fidelity fluid–structure interaction (FSI) analysis with improved computational efficiency. This study develops a partitioned framework that couples large-eddy simulation (LES) of the axial flow with a transfer-matrix and modal-superposition structural solver, where the fuel rod is modelled as an Euler–Bernoulli beam. The framework is verified on a clamped–clamped rod by comparison with an in-house finite element method (FEM) beam model, the Ansys APDL BEAM4 element, and the SOLID187 solid element. The first three natural frequencies and the root-mean-square (RMS) displacements at L /4, L /2, and 3 L /4 agree to within approximately 1% across all comparisons. The predicted displacement spectra accurately reproduce the first three structural modes and their orbital trajectories. Compared with the in-house FEM, the beam-based TMM structural solver reduces the structural computation time by about a factor of four at comparable accuracy for the present single-rod case, and its advantage is expected to grow for multi-span, grid-supported rod configurations, since the size of the transfer-matrix system is independent of the number of spans and lumped supports. Validation against the axial-flow-induced vibration experiment of Cioncolini et al. yields predictions of the first-mode frequency and RMS free-end displacement within 2.4% and 3% of the measurements, respectively.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112868','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112868',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','CN','CN','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112850','Bayesian inference for 3D activity reconstruction combining X-ray Computed Tomography and Angular Segmented Gamma Scanning','X-선 전산화 단층 촬영과 각도 세그먼트 감마 스캐닝을 결합한 3D 활동 재구성에 대한 베이지안 추론','Annals of Nuclear Energy','0306-4549','Victor J. Casas-Molina; Eric Laloy; Bart Rogiers; Tom Dhaene; Ivo Couckuyt','2026-09-25','','2027-02','연구논문','방사선·계측','This study evaluates a Bayesian variational framework for 3D activity reconstruction in 220-liter radioactive waste drums using Angular Segmented Gamma Scanning (ASGS) and attenuation information derived from X-ray Computed Tomography (CT). Computations are made tractable using stochastic variational inference together with a multi-resolution spatial prior for the inferred activities. A CT-to-LAC model is developed to convert CT-derived gray levels into energy-dependent linear attenuation coefficients, allowing the forward model to be constructed under experimental conditions. Tests were performed using homogeneous and heterogeneous mock-ups, with point and linear sources made of either 241 Am or 152 Eu. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first experimental validation of Bayesian 3D activity reconstruction from coupled ASGS and CT measurements for radiological waste characterization. Results show that the approach recovers spatial activity distributions broadly consistent with known source locations. Regarding total activity, for the multi-line isotope 152 Eu, the true activity is recovered within the posterior distribution and the posterior mean outperforms conventional averaging for this multi-line isotope. Posterior mean relative errors ranged from 0.89% to 8.00% across the homogeneous and heterogeneous mock-ups, compared with errors up to 27.19% for the conventional averaged estimate. Posterior standard deviations on total activity were typically about 4%–6% of the posterior mean, indicating well-constrained estimates. In contrast, for 241 Am, reconstructed from the single 59.54 keV emission line, posterior mean relative errors ranged from 26.27% to 42.29%, reflecting combined single-line non-identifiability and stronger sensitivity to attenuation-model errors at low energy.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112850','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112850',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','BE','BE','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106618','Unfolding the extraction and coordination behavior of actinides by cyclohexyl phosphonate derivatives','사이클로헥실포스포네이트 유도체에 의한 악티니드의 추출 및 배위 거동 전개','Progress in Nuclear Energy','0149-1970','Puchakayala Rajani; G. Jegan; Balija Sreenivasulu; K. Prabhakara Rao; Vemuri Lakshmi Ganesh; Gopinadhanpillai Gopakumar; C.V.S. Brahmananda Rao','2026-09-24','','2027-01','연구논문','핵연료·재료','The effect of branching in the carbon chain of phosphonate-based extractants on the selective extraction of actinides from fission products of spent nuclear fuel has been investigated experimentally and through quantum chemical calculations. Although phosphonate-based extractants have been known for a long time, only a few studies have reported the extraction of actinides using cyclohexyl phosphonates. In this study, two dialkylcyclohexyl phosphonate-based extractants, namely di- iso -butyl-cyclohexyl phosphonate (DiBCyHeP) and di- sec -butyl-cyclohexyl phosphonate (DsBCyHeP), were evaluated for the extraction of actinides. The difference in actinide extraction arising from variations in the alkyl chain substituents of the extractant molecules were further examined using density functional theory (DFT) computations to investigate the electronic structure and complexation behavior of the ligands with actinides such as U(VI) and Pu(IV). The extraction performance of DiBCyHeP and DsBCyHeP for actinides and fission products was compared with that of the commercially used PUREX solvent (1.1 M TBP/ n -DD) under identical conditions. The experimentally measured distribution ratios for actinides showed good agreement with the computed binding affinities of the corresponding metal-ligand complexes.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106618','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106618',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','IN','IN','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106622','Integrating artificial intelligence into nuclear reactor safety: ML-augmented CFD, AI-enabled PSA, and cybersecurity','원자로 안전에 인공 지능 통합: ML 증강 CFD, AI 지원 PSA 및 사이버 보안','Progress in Nuclear Energy','0149-1970','Luiz Umberto Rodrigues Sica','2026-09-24','','2027-01','연구논문','열수력·유체','This review provides a structured synthesis of how artificial intelligence (AI) is reshaping safety-critical analysis in nuclear power plants through three complementary technical domains and cross-cutting enablers. First, we examine machine learning (ML) augmentations to computational fluid dynamics (CFD) and reduced-order thermal–hydraulic models, focusing on boiling and critical heat flux (CHF) closures, interfacial transport, transient emulation, and containment-scale mixing. Particular attention is given to physics-constrained surrogates, verification, validation, and uncertainty quantification (VVUQ), and best-estimate plus uncertainty (BEPU) practices that enable credible acceleration of high-fidelity analyses. Second, we review AI integration within probabilistic safety assessment (PSA), covering Bayesian and hybrid graphical models, dynamic event and fault trees, human reliability analysis enhanced by learning-based support, and digital-twin frameworks that embed online updating of risk metrics. Third, we examine cybersecurity in digital instrumentation and control (I&C) systems, including threat pathways, AI-enabled detection and response, adversarial model robustness, and the coupling of cyber–physical consequences with PSA. These technical strands are complemented by recent developments in uncertainty quantification and resilient AI, together with a dedicated assessment of multi-unit and multi-hazard PSA covering inter-unit and inter-module dependencies, shared structures, systems, components, and recovery resources, multi-unit accident sequences, and single, combined, and cascading external hazards. Across all domains, we consolidate comparative evidence, highlight regulatory and explainability requirements, and identify open challenges linked to data scarcity, plant heterogeneity across small modular reactor (SMR), pressurized water reactor (PWR), and boiling water reactor (BWR) designs, and physics–data integration. The outcome is a consolidated map of methods and datasets, together with a lifecycle governance framework for AI-enabled PSA that links intended use, data and model qualification, PSA integration, configuration control, operational monitoring, requalification, and human oversight to auditable decision gates.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106622','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106622',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','BR','BR','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106628','Experimental study on flow pattern evolution characteristics of gas-liquid two-phase flow in a 3×3 rod bundle channel','3 × 3 로드 번들 채널에서 기체-액체 2-상 유동의 유동 패턴 진화 특성에 관한 실험적 연구','Progress in Nuclear Energy','0149-1970','Jianchuang Sun; Songqi Kang; Junjie Peng; Liang Guan; Deshun Chen; Wenchao Zhang; Wentao Wu; Weihua Cai','2026-09-24','','2027-01','연구논문','열수력·유체','To address the insufficient understanding of two-phase flow characteristics in the small-scale rod bundle channels, this study takes air-water two-phase flow as the research object and carries out a systematic experimental investigation in a 3×3 rod bundle channel. High-speed imaging is combined with a self-developed dual-fiber optical probe system. Flow pattern evolution, local phase distribution, and the time-domain and frequency-domain characteristics of probe signals are obtained under different operating conditions. Five typical flow patterns are identified from the optical probe signals, including bubbly flow, cap-bubbly flow, cap-turbulent flow, churn flow, and finely dispersed flow. A flow regime map is established using a combined identification criterion. Compared with existing transition criteria for vertical rod bundles, the general sequence of flow-regime evolution is consistent, while noticeable differences are observed in individual transition boundaries. The deviation of the finely dispersed-flow boundary from the existing criterion decreases as the gas superficial velocity increases. These differences are associated with rod-bundle geometry, bubble coalescence and breakup, and local disturbances. The local measurements show a core-peaked radial distribution of void fraction, while the radial profile of mean bubble velocity remains relatively uniform. Frequency-domain analysis of the probe signals reveals distinct spectral characteristics for different flow patterns. Bubbly and finely dispersed flows exhibit broadband spectra, whereas cap-bubbly, cap-turbulent, and churn flows show dominant low-frequency peaks with spatial variations.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106628','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106628',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','CN;NZ','CN','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.net.2026.104739','Temperature-Dependent Corrosion of Structural Alloys for Molten Salt Reactors in NaCl-MgCl2 Molten Salt','NaCl-MgCl2 용융염 중 용융염 원자로용 구조 합금의 온도 의존적 부식','Nuclear Engineering and Technology','1738-5733','Seok Min Yoon; Eun-Young Choi; Chang Hwa Lee; Taeho Kim','2026-09-24','','2026-09','연구논문','핵연료·재료','The corrosion behavior of Inconel 600, SS316H, and Hastelloy C276 was investigated in molten NaCl-MgCl 2 eutectic salt by 500 h static immersion tests at 550 °C and 650 °C. Mass change measurements, SEM-EDS, and XRD were combined to relate alloy degradation to surface and subsurface microstructural evolution. At 550 °C, all three alloys showed relatively shallow, surface-confined attack without detectable crystalline corrosion products, indicating dissolution-dominated behavior. At 650 °C, corrosion became strongly alloy-dependent: Inconel 600 developed a continuous MgO-rich surface deposit and limited subsurface damage with net mass gain, SS316H experienced dissolution-dominated attack with deep void formation and no protective deposit, and Hastelloy C276 formed a thick but non-uniform spinel layer coupled with significant internal degradation. These results demonstrate a temperature-driven transition from simple dissolution to mixed dissolution-precipitation and show that nominal Cr content alone is insufficient to predict corrosion resistance in molten chlorides, underscoring the need to consider full alloy chemistry and salt impurity effects in materials selection for chloride-based molten salt systems.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.net.2026.104739','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.net.2026.104739',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','KR;SE','KR','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.net.2026.104737','Technical reviewers for Nuclear Engineering and Technology, 2026','2026년 원자력 공학 및 기술 기술 기술 검토자','Nuclear Engineering and Technology','1738-5733','N.A. Man Gyun','2026-09-24','','2026-09','리뷰·관점','기타 원자력','','','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.net.2026.104737','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.net.2026.104737',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','','','OpenAlex: country unavailable');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.net.2026.104738','Radionuclide production cross-sections from proton-induced reactions on natural iron up to 100 MeV','최대 100 MeV의 천연 철에 대한 양성자 유도 반응으로부터의 방사성 핵종 생성 단면','Nuclear Engineering and Technology','1738-5733','Sung-Chul Yang; Dal-Ho Moon; Myung-Hwan Jung; Young Seok Hwang','2026-09-24','','2026-09','연구논문','핵연료·재료','The production cross-sections of 55,56,57 Co, 52g Fe, 52g,54 Mn, 48,51 Cr, and 48 V from nat Fe(p,x) reactions were determined at the Korea Multi-purpose Accelerator Complex (KOMAC) for proton energies up to 100 MeV. Stacked-foil irradiations were followed by off-line γ-ray spectrometry using an HPGe detector. The proton beam flux was determined using the 27 Al(p,x) 24 Na monitor reaction. The present data combined with our earlier measurements using a 57 MeV proton beam provide excitation functions covering the energy range from the reaction thresholds up to 100 MeV. The excitation functions obtained in this study are generally consistent with the available experimental data in the literature, whereas the level of agreement with the TENDL-2025 evaluated nuclear data library varies depending on the radionuclide and proton energy region.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.net.2026.104738','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.net.2026.104738',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','KR','KR','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.nucengdes.2026.115222','Waste classification of PWR concrete biological shield: Impact of detector-window cavities on neutron streaming','PWR 콘크리트 생물학적 차폐물의 폐기물 분류: 검출기-창문 공동이 중성자 스트리밍에 미치는 영향','Nuclear Engineering and Design','0029-5493','Ryoma Nishiyama; Naoko Watanabe; Kenichi Tanaka; Tamotsu Kozaki','2026-09-24','','2026-12','연구논문','원자로물리·해석','Concrete biological shields (CBSs) of large pressurized water reactors (PWRs) yield a significant amount of waste upon decommissioning, and neutron-transport and activation calculations are useful tools for evaluating their radioactivity distributions. However, the impact of the geometric modeling fidelity of the calculation models on decommissioning waste management is not fully understood. This study focused on detector-window (DW) cavities in the CBS and investigated the effects of DW on radioactive waste classification for a representative 4-loop PWR using the three-dimensional continuous-energy Monte Carlo transport simulation code PHITS and its post-processing multi-group activation code DCHAIN-PHITS. The air-filled DW cavity formed a low-attenuation streaming path, producing higher neutron flux near the cavity end and in the deep CBS region. These neutron-flux characteristics were reflected in the spatial distribution of activation products with a bulge around the DW and thus in the waste-class boundaries. Applying Japanese regulatory criteria, the DW cavity produced a bulge in the L3 (VLLW)-clearance boundary in the DW direction, and this effect persisted throughout the cooling time. In contrast, the influence of the DW cavity on the L2 (LLW) region was limited, because the L2 waste diminished as 60 Co decayed. These results demonstrated that the localized deep activation near DW openings is significant enough for considering waste minimization through clearance.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nucengdes.2026.115222','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nucengdes.2026.115222',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','JP;US','JP','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.nucengdes.2026.115216','CFD assessment of heat transfer and frictional pressure loss correlations for molten salt reactors with non-condensable gases','비응축 가스와 용융 염 원자로의 열전달 및 마찰 압력 손실 상관 관계에 대한 CFD 평가','Nuclear Engineering and Design','0029-5493','Andrew Finton Buchanan; Qingqing Liu','2026-09-24','','2026-12','연구논문','열수력·유체','','','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nucengdes.2026.115216','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nucengdes.2026.115216',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','','','OpenAlex: country unavailable');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112872','Temperature–based quantitative detection of micro–internal leakage in heat–cycle isolation valves of nuclear power plants','원자력 발전소의 열 사이클 분리 밸브에서 미세 내부 누출의 온도 기반 정량적 감지','Annals of Nuclear Energy','0306-4549','Yuanjiao Li; Zhaoguang Zhang; Qinglei Jiang; Wang Yong; Jingjun Lou','2026-09-24','','2027-02','연구논문','열수력·유체','The internal leakage of heat-cycle isolation valves in nuclear power plants impairs plant economics and may trigger environmental contamination or safety issues due to medium release. Non-destructive, on-line and quantitative detection methods are therefore urgently required. Among existing techniques, the temperature method is the most promising for quantitative on-line leakage detection of high-temperature valves; however, the coupled multi-physics nature of micro-leakage and their invisibility make both identification and quantification challenging. Taking actual plant pressure and temperature as boundary conditions, artificial valve defects were designed and a fluid-thermal-solid coupled CFD model was built. Temperature-characteristic parameters extracted from the simulated outer-wall temperature field were correlated with leakage rate by combining the least-squares method with multiple linear regression. Experimental validation showed predicted leakage rates agree with measured values within a mean relative error of 6% and the minimum detectable leakage is 2 L/h, outperforming traditional models. The temperature-based predictor therefore offers practical value for enhanced online monitoring and predictive maintenance in nuclear power plants.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112872','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112872',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','CN','CN','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112851','NUCLEA: A thermodynamic database validated for severe accident codes','NUCLEA: 심각한 사고 코드에 대해 검증된 열역학 데이터베이스','Annals of Nuclear Energy','0306-4549','M. Barrachin; B. Michel; B. Piar; E. Fischer','2026-09-24','','2027-02','연구논문','안전·사고·리스크','The objective of this article is to present an overview of the validation of the NUCLEA thermodynamic database through a comparative analysis with experimental data. These examples are selected from important experimental programs (notably OECD MASCA, EC ENTHALPY) in order to cover various important topics for improving severe accident codes, especially regarding the progression of corium inside or outside the vessel. The comparison between calculations and measurements is discussed, and the main lessons learned from this extensive validation work are summarized, along with an assessment of the current relevance of the NUCLEA database concerning the thermodynamics of corium. Based on these results, the validity domain of NUCLEA is defined, and prospects for future improvement are proposed.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112851','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112851',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','FR','FR','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106592','Molten salt reactors: Design concepts, global development status, and technical challenges','용융염 원자로: 설계 개념, 글로벌 개발 상태 및 기술적 과제','Progress in Nuclear Energy','0149-1970','Seda Yilmaz; Shripad T. Revankar','2026-09-23','','2027-01','연구논문','열수력·유체','Molten salt reactors (MSRs) have re-emerged as a promising class of advanced nuclear systems due to their potential for high-temperature operation, low-pressure operation, and flexible fuel-cycle strategies. In contrast to conventional solid-fuel reactors, MSRs employ either circulating liquid fuel or molten-salt coolants, enabling strong coupling among neutronics, thermal-hydraulics, chemistry, and fuel-composition evolution during operation. This review presents a comprehensive overview of the state of the art in MSR technology, with particular emphasis on liquid-fueled systems and thorium-based fuel cycles. It discusses key aspects of MSR development, including historical evolution, reactor classification, core design and neutronics, salt chemistry and materials compatibility, thermal-hydraulics, and multiphysics modeling. In addition, MSR-specific topics such as fuel-cycle and reprocessing strategies, safety behavior, source-term characteristics, and global development activities are examined. Particular attention is given to the interdependence of design choices, highlighting how fuel form, neutron spectrum, salt chemistry, and fuel-cycle strategy collectively influence reactor performance, technical complexity, and deployment feasibility. As a central synthesis, this review identifies a key tradeoff between fuel utilization and system complexity across near-term and long-term MSR concepts. Finally, it concludes that continued MSR progress will depend on integrated demonstrations of reactor physics, chemistry, materials performance, fuel processing, safety behavior, and system-level operation, while also addressing the major technical and regulatory challenges that remain for future advancement.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106592','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106592',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','US','US','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106620','Assessment of a twisted double-tube latent heat storage system from the perspectives of the first and second laws of thermodynamics throughout the melting and solidification processes','용융 및 응고 공정 전반에 걸친 열역학 제1법칙 및 제2법칙의 관점에서 비틀린 이중관 잠열 저장 시스템의 평가','Progress in Nuclear Energy','0149-1970','Abderrahmane Aissa; Ali Alkhafaji; Dheyaa J. Jasim; Narinderjit Singh Sawaran Singh; Sorour Alotaibi; Mahmut Taner; Shaghayegh Baghaei; Soheil Salahshour','2026-09-23','','2027-01','리뷰·관점','열수력·유체','The current study numerically investigates the impact of twist angle on the performance of a twisted double-tube latent heat storage (LHS) system, evaluating first- and second-law efficiencies during melting and solidification. Water serves as the heat transfer fluid (HTF) circulating within the inner tube, while the phase change material (PCM) RT35 is situated in the annular space. Performance is evaluated by examining the PCM temperature, liquid fraction, and the rates of thermal and frictional entropy generation over time. The findings indicate that lower twist angles result in reduced melting and solidification durations. A notable exception is the 360° case during melting, which achieves the shortest melting time among all configurations due to the dominance of vortex-enhanced convective heat transfer over the minor stagnation-zone penalty at this twist angle. Frictional entropy generation is consistently negligible compared to thermal entropy generation. Thermal entropy generation exhibits an increasing-then-decreasing trend during solidification, with peak values of 7.8 W/m 3 /K and 1.8 W/m 3 /K recorded during solidification and melting, respectively. At higher twist angles, both phase-change durations are extended.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106620','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106620',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','DZ;IQ;MY;KW;TR;AZ','DZ','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1080/00295450.2026.2716592','Updates to Dynamic Functions in the GAMMA-PC Methodology for Dry Storage Loading Optimization','건식 스토리지 로딩 최적화를 위한 GAMMA-PC 방법론의 동적 기능 업데이트','Nuclear Technology','0029-5450','Kristy Yancey Spencer; Peter Stefanovic','2026-09-23','2026-09-23','2026-09-23','연구논문','열수력·유체','This article presents updates to the Greedy Adaptive Multi-Objective Memetic Algorithm with Partial Clustering (GAMMA-PC), an evolutionary algorithm designed to select used nuclear fuel assemblies for dry storage over a reactor’s lifetime. The algorithm simultaneously minimizes the number of canisters, the average initial heat load, and the maximum time to transportation readiness. The U.S. Department of Energy developed GAMMA-PC for integration with the STANDARDS used nuclear fuel data and analysis tool. The tool was recently updated to enable user-defined loading schedules. This functionality was tested by comparing GAMMA-PC’s performance using the default continuous timeline versus the new scheduling-friendly timeline.Comanche Peak Nuclear Power Plant was chosen as a test case. For the discrete schedule, five dates were provided from 2019 to 2025 followed by a loading frequency of every 24 months. GAMMA-PC performed as well as or better than status quo (i.e. the default continuous timeline) when using the discrete timeline. A staggered optimization approach was also tested to simulate how a utility might use the software. Each stage of the optimization ran one-fifth of the number of function evaluations, resulting in a solution that was comparable to the single longer run. This suggests that GAMMA-PC might not need as long to converge toward the Pareto front as originally expected. These improvements add a new functionality and demonstrate the continued usefulness of GAMMA-PC in dry storage loading and scheduling optimization.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1080/00295450.2026.2716592','https://doi.org/10.1080/00295450.2026.2716592',0,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','US','US','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1080/00295450.2026.2724787','Proposition of New Structures to the “Don’t Know” Classification Applied to the Problem of Diagnostic of Abnormal Situations in Nuclear Power Plants','원자력 발전소의 이상 상황 진단 문제에 적용되는 "모름" 분류에 대한 새로운 구조의 제안','Nuclear Technology','0029-5450','João Pedro Perin Nunes; Andressa dos Santos Nicolau','2026-09-23','2026-09-23','2026-09-23','연구논문','AI·디지털','Machine learning, a field that has grown in the past years, includes applications in the Nuclear Engineering domain. The problem of diagnostic of abnormal situations (PDAS) can be seen as an application of the separation problem of machine learning to help operators understand the signals in the control room in nuclear power plants. To avoid returning wrong responses that could worsen the judgment of the operator, a methodology called “Don’t know” has been developed and applied to the PDAS problem. This article introduces new geometrical structures with the objective of upgrading the Don’t know model proposed in the literature, improving its accuracy, precision, recall, and specificity using a new proposition of algorithm H-QEA, based on the quantum-inspired evolutionary algorithm, to optimize the classification of events.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1080/00295450.2026.2724787','https://doi.org/10.1080/00295450.2026.2724787',0,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','BR','BR','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1080/00295450.2026.2727776','Digital Twin–Driven Nuclear Power Plant Health Monitoring, Diagnostics, and Prognostics','디지털 트윈 기반 원자력 발전소 건강 모니터링, 진단 및 예후','Nuclear Technology','0029-5450','Deepak Haste; Sudipto Ghoshal; Christopher Norton; Jamie Coble; Vivek Agarwal','2026-09-23','2026-09-23','2026-09-23','연구논문','안전·사고·리스크','Nuclear energy has the potential to significantly change the energy production landscape in the United States, meeting the nation’s energy demands in a sustainable and environmentally responsible manner. However, the economics associated with operating the aging nuclear power plants (NPPs) require improvement while maintaining their high availability. The U.S. Department of Energy’s Office of Nuclear Energy has been focusing on key research and development initiatives for the advancement of nuclear energy, such as technologies and solutions to improve reliability, sustain the safety, and extend the life of current reactors.This paper highlights the research to deliver an integrated decision support system for NPPs to provide improvements to the operations and maintenance (O&M) costs across the complete life cycle of a light water reactor, from design and deployment to O&M. The underlying driver of the improved O&M costs is our hybrid approach with a data-driven analysis and model-based systems engineering approach that utilizes a common model for all sustainment phases.The research team devised an approach to map anomalies discovered through data-driven or physics-based models to specific root causes using digital twins. Any previously undiscovered anomaly can be augmented in the system model via a continuous improvement process that allows analysts to view cause-effect relationships for the anomaly within a model-based perspective and helps associate failure causes to the anomaly.The research team derived degradation indicators based on the hybrid approach and applied them to data gathered from the circulating water system of an operating NPP covering a period of 14 years. The indicators performed moderately well owing to the lack of data quality and consistency, with a false positive rate ranging from 0% to 33% and a false negative rate ranging from 11% to 50%. This technology has the potential to reduce O&M costs and increase NPP reliability and lifespan.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1080/00295450.2026.2727776','https://doi.org/10.1080/00295450.2026.2727776',0,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','US','US','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.net.2026.104731','Design and Optimization of a Low-Background Shielding System for Gaseous Tritium Measurement','기체 삼중수소 측정을 위한 저배경 차폐 시스템 설계 및 최적화','Nuclear Engineering and Technology','1738-5733','Shiliang Liu; Jinhui Qu; Yan Zhang; Linseng Yang; Haoran Zhang; Bao Wang; Xuandi Hu; Jianqiang Xu; Jun Gong; Renbo Wang','2026-09-23','','2026-09','연구논문','원자로물리·해석','Sensitive measurement of residual tritium in 3 He is important for radiation safety and product quality, but low-energy background limits the measurement of low-activity gaseous tritium. The contribution of multiple environmental and shielding-related background sources is difficult to quantify separately. This study proposes a source-resolved Monte Carlo approach in which the environmental radiation field is constrained by an ambient dose rate of 0.1 μSv/h, and individual background sources are modeled and quantified separately. The resulting source contributions are used to identify the dominant backgrounds and optimize a graded Pb/Cu/PMMA shielding structure. A lead thickness of about 7 cm, together with 1 mm Cu and 3 mm PMMA liners, was selected to suppress the dominant γ-ray and characteristic X-ray backgrounds. The optimized system yielded simulated and experimental background count rates of 0.145 ± 0.003 and 0.183 ± 0.014 cps, respectively, corresponding to MDAs of 0.35 and 0.44 Bq/L (@60min). The results demonstrate that source-resolved background quantification provides a practical basis for shielding optimization and low-background gaseous tritium measurement.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.net.2026.104731','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.net.2026.104731',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','CN','CN','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.net.2026.104716','Automating real-time recovery from radiation-induced errors with byte-sized corrections','바이트 크기의 수정으로 방사선 유발 오류로부터 실시간 복구 자동화','Nuclear Engineering and Technology','1738-5733','Mehmet Alp Şarkışla; Rauf Ozan Akpınar; Berkay Yazıcı; Bora Akgün','2026-09-23','','2027-02','연구논문','방사선·계측','Radiation-induced errors in high-energy physics detectors threaten data integrity and uptime, posing significant challenges for reliable operation during the future high-luminosity LHC runs. With over 150,000 ASICs to be deployed in the CMS HGCAL front-end, even a very low per-chip error probability results in a non-negligible failure rate. This work presents a firmware-based, byte-level autorecovery mechanism for the CMS HGCAL Phase-2 front-end electronics, capable of detecting transient upset-related errors and restoring chip operation in real time without requiring a full system reset, ensuring data acquisition continues uninterrupted. The system, implemented in FPGA logic and validated using front-end emulation, executes recovery actions within 4.7 µs, corresponding to the measured interval from the data acquisition signal to packet arrival. Overall, the hardware approach is measured to recover approximately 73.5 ms faster than software-based intervention. The results demonstrate robust, autonomous operation in radiation-prone environments while preserving continuous data acquisition.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.net.2026.104716','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.net.2026.104716',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','CH;TR','CH','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.nucengdes.2026.115215','An advanced solution for nuclear safety I&amp;C software: Deep integration of model-driven development and formal methods','원자력 안전 I&amp;C 소프트웨어를 위한 고급 솔루션: 모델 중심 개발 및 공식 방법의 깊은 통합','Nuclear Engineering and Design','0029-5493','Rongbin Hou; Quan Ma; Mingxing Liu; Yang Zhao; Fei Yang; Nan Li; Yongtao Zhang','2026-09-23','','2026-12','연구논문','안전·사고·리스크','Instrumentation and control (I&C) systems constitute the central nervous system of a nuclear power plant. In digital I&C systems, software is the core carrier of functionality and may be regarded as the “cognition” of the plant. With the rapid growth in both the complexity and scale of software functions, software reliability—especially for safety-class software—has become a critical challenge. Formal methods offer a promising solution to this challenge; however, in the nuclear domain, their application remains fragmented and exploratory, lacking a systematic methodology and integrated framework. In this paper, we propose a deeply integrated framework that combines model-driven development with formal methods across the entire software lifecycle. The framework addresses, in a formal and unified manner: (1) the precision of software requirements and design specifications, (2) behavioral consistency between software design and implementation, and (3) conformance of software design to formalized requirements. The framework integrates formal requirements specification, synchronous dataflow-based design modeling, formal verification, and semantics-preserving code generation into a traceable workflow. It is implemented in the LoongMeta toolchain and evaluated using representative functions from the Hualong One (HPR1000) reactor protection system and the Linglong One (ACP100) power control system. The evaluation achieved an automatic code generation rate of 90%, 100% behavioral consistency between the design models and generated C code, and an approximately 30% improvement in overall engineering efficiency. Overall, these results show that the deep integration of model-driven development and formal methods provides a practical, traceable, and quantitatively assessable approach to quality assurance for safety-critical nuclear I&C software.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nucengdes.2026.115215','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nucengdes.2026.115215',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','CN','CN','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112847','The fission matrix method for criticality search in Heat-Pipe Micro Reactors','Heat-Pipe Micro Reactors에서 임계 검색을 위한 핵분열 매트릭스 방법','Annals of Nuclear Energy','0306-4549','Christian Vita; Nicolò Abrate; Valerio Mascolino; William Walters; Nicolas Stauff; Fausto Franceschini; Sandra Dulla','2026-09-23','','2027-02','연구논문','열수력·유체','In this work the Fission Matrix (FM) method is proposed to conduct the neutronics analysis of a Heat-Pipe Micro Reactor (HPMR). The aim of this model is to support the real-time modelling of heat-pipe-based microreactor designs, which have recently gained significant industrial interest. A database of FMs is generated through different criticality calculations, each of them considering a different core configuration (temperature profile and control drums position). The database is then interpolated to generate, in principle, the results for any arbitrary core layout. In this study the accuracy decrease of the FM method in the presence of a temperature difference between the core and the reflector is highlighted and addressed. The accuracy of the method is assessed through comparison with reference Serpent 2 simulations. Different core temperature profiles and control drum positions are tested. Finally, leveraging the computational efficiency and high accuracy of the FM, a criticality search algorithm is developed. Overall, the results show very good agreement with the corresponding Serpent 2 reference cases, while requiring only a very short computational time.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112847','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112847',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','IT;US','IT','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112855','Design, dynamic analysis, and optimization of a hybrid nuclear-storage power system for mobile applications','모바일 애플리케이션을 위한 하이브리드 원자력 저장 전력 시스템의 설계, 동적 분석 및 최적화','Annals of Nuclear Energy','0306-4549','Mengfei Niu; Zhipeng Zhang; Haocheng Zhang; Chenglong Wang; Wenxi Tian','2026-09-23','','2027-02','연구논문','열수력·유체','With the rapid advancement of heat pipe-cooled reactor (HPR) technology, the integration of mobile nuclear power with energy storage offers a promising solution for energy supply in remote and off-grid scenarios. However, existing research predominantly focuses on stationary systems, lacking comprehensive investigation into the dynamic coupling of HPRs and hybrid storage in mobile environments. To address this gap, this paper proposes a novel hybrid nuclear-storage power system (HNSPS) integrating an HPR with a lithium-ion battery and supercapacitor for mobile applications. A high-fidelity system model is developed in MATLAB/Simulink, incorporating a nuclear power generation system, hybrid energy storage, and control models. A fully active parallel topology is employed for the storage subsystem, utilizing lithium-ion batteries for baseload and supercapacitors for transient power fluctuation mitigation. Simulation results demonstrate robust synergistic operation, characterized by excellent dynamic response and energy management performance. Parameter optimization indicates that the lithium battery rated voltage (determined by series configuration) should balance cycle life and manufacturing cost, while its response time should be extended for high-frequency operation and shortened for high-power-demand scenarios. For the supercapacitor, an initial voltage of 100–200 V and a series number of approximately 200 are recommended to balance transient power capability, safety, and cost. This work validates the feasibility and reliability of the proposed HNSPS for mobile scenarios, providing a theoretical reference for the deployment of mobile nuclear energy applications.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112855','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112855',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','CN','CN','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106595','SCALE validation for pebble bed reactor applications using the HTR-PROTEUS benchmark suite','HTR-PROTEUS 벤치마크 제품군을 사용하여 페블베드 원자로 적용에 대한 스케일 검증','Progress in Nuclear Energy','0149-1970','Donny Hartanto; Rabab R. Elzohery; Yves E. Robert; Friederike Bostelmann; William Wieselquist','2026-09-22','','2027-01','연구논문','원자로물리·해석','Reliable simulation of graphite-moderated pebble bed reactors requires validation against integral experiments. This work validates the SCALE development version (SCALE 7.0 Beta 12) against the HTR-PROTEUS benchmark suite from the International Handbook of Evaluated Reactor Physics Benchmark Experiments (IRPhE). Eleven critical configurations were analyzed, including hexagonal close-packed (HCP), columnar hexagonal point-on-point (CHPOP), and nominally random pebble-bed arrangements, with variations in moderator-to-fuel ratio and simulated water-ingress conditions. Criticality calculations were performed with SCALE’s Shift Monte Carlo code using multiple nuclear data libraries in both continuous-energy and multigroup modes and with explicit pebble modeling. Recent Shift enhancements in the mesh acceleration for such stochastic geometries made the simulations efficient. In addition to criticality, the effective delayed neutron fraction and selected control rod worths were evaluated. Nuclear data sensitivity and uncertainty analyses were also performed. The calculations reproduced the main benchmark trends, with both ENDF/B-VIII.0 and the preliminary SCALE-processed ENDF/B-VIII.1 continuous-energy libraries yielding smaller criticality biases than ENDF/B-VII.1. For ENDF/B-VII.1 and ENDF/B-VIII.0, the propagated nuclear-data-induced uncertainty in 𝑘 e f f remained near 0.7%. The ENDF/B-VIII.1 analysis was limited to the continuous-energy 𝑘 e f f comparison. Overall, these results support the use of SCALE for high-fidelity analysis of graphite-moderated pebble bed reactor systems.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106595','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106595',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','US','US','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106616','Reaction rate of high-temperature sodium with air based on sodium heat pipe leakage','소듐 히트 파이프 누출을 기반으로 한 고온 소듐과 공기의 반응 속도','Progress in Nuclear Energy','0149-1970','Shuai Liu; Mingming Kang; Yuan Zhou; Yuan Yuan','2026-09-22','','2027-01','연구논문','열수력·유체','In sodium heat pipe reactors and sodium-cooled fast reactors, sodium leakage can initiate sodium fires. High-temperature sodium vapor reacts with air, potentially leading to deflagration. The reaction rate between high-temperature (900°C) sodium vapor and air constitutes a fundamental parameter for the safety design in high-temperature sodium facilities. This study conducted an experimental simulation of a rupture in a high-temperature sodium heat pipe, allowing leaked sodium vapor to undergo deflagration in air with varying humidity levels (0 to 19 mg/L), yielding a deflagration overpressure range of 0.074 ± 0.017 MPa. Temperature and pressure data monitored within the reaction chamber were used to determine the mass of sodium consumed during combustion. By combining these measurements with numerical simulations that established the combustion duration, the reaction rate for sodium vapor with ambient air at approximately 900°C was determined to be 1 . 3 + 0 . 5 ‐ 0 . 4 kg/s.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106616','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106616',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','CN','CN','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106623','Expanding the capabilities of physics-informed neural networks through parametrisation for the neutron diffusion equation','중성자 확산 방정식에 대한 매개변수를 통해 물리 정보 신경망의 기능 확장','Progress in Nuclear Energy','0149-1970','Hasnain Gulzar; Dzianis Litskevich; Seddon Atkinson; Anna Detkina; Abdo Ez Aldeen','2026-09-22','','2027-01','연구논문','원자로물리·해석','Physics-Informed Neural Networks (PINNs) have emerged as a promising alternative for solving systems governed by partial differential equations (PDEs), such as the neutron diffusion equation. Here, conventional PINNs assume fixed PDE parameters, however, in reality, the material parameters inside a nuclear reactor core vary continuously. The variation renders the conventional fixed parameters PINNs unsuitable as the models must be retrained. To address this challenge, a parametric PINNs model is built so inferences can be made on various material properties without the need for retraining. This article aims to demonstrates the capabilities of PINNs in resolving the neutron diffusion equation with fixed parameters in both one-dimensional and two-dimensional geometries and compared to the reference finite difference solver. Then, the functionality of PINNs is extended by training the network across a range of material parameters pertaining to real physical changes in reactor conditions generated through OpenMC. The model is then verified against a reference solver on one-hundred random material properties within the OpenMC generated range, showing good agreement with the reference solution but with a vastly accelerated calculation routine.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106623','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106623',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','GB','GB','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106605','Development of a modal reduced-order model of xenon oscillations in PWRs','PWR에서 제논 진동의 모달 감소 순서 모델 개발','Progress in Nuclear Energy','0149-1970','Kristoffer Tofveson Pedersen; Christophe Demazière; Paolo Vinai','2026-09-22','','2027-01','연구논문','원자로물리·해석','This work presents a modal reduced-order modelling approach to predict the stability of xenon oscillations in large Pressurised Water Reactors (PWRs). The model, labelled 2G-HET, is developed and compared to a simplified model (2G-HOM). The 2G-HET model takes the two-energy group and three-dimensional macroscopic cross sections, homogenised at the assembly or sub-assembly level, as well as the reactor geometry, as input. It calculates spatial eigenmodes and their coupled time-dependent amplitudes for neutron flux, iodine, and xenon concentrations. In contrast, the 2G-HOM model uses cross sections homogenised over the entire core-reflector region and analytically calculated eigenmodes.The 2G-HOM and 2G-HET are compared against each other based on simulated cross-section data from the Ringhals-4 PWR in Sweden. The analysis shows the significant influence of mode coupling on power and axial offset deviations, with 2G-HET capturing detailed spatial dynamics absent in 2G-HOM. Specifically, the heterogeneous treatment reveals couplings between even and odd modes, not present in the homogeneous model. In a case study, the 2G-HET model showed stable decaying oscillations with modal periods in the interval [31.5;39.3] h. Comparisons with 2G-HOM over a range of power feedback coefficient strengths (FB-scaling) show similar asymptotic behaviour between the two models. However, 2G-HOM have an onset of stable oscillations at 25% lower FB-scaling value compared to 2G-HET. It also consistently displays faster decaying oscillations compared to 2G-HET.The results highlight that high spatial resolution plays an important role when simulating xenon oscillations following reactor perturbations using three dimensional modal expansion models. The findings show that 2G-HET captures more features of the system, which substantially alters the stability prediction and mode-coupling behaviour. Capturing these features has potential applications in ensuring safe and flexible reactor operation by capturing more complex dynamics of xenon oscillations which cannot be reproduced in simpler models.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106605','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106605',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','','','OpenAlex: country unavailable');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1080/00295450.2026.2726706','Mobile Low-Dwell Time Robotic Radiation Mapping Using Auto-Regressive Multi-Fidelity Gaussian Processes (ARGP) in Saturated Radiological Environments','포화 방사선 환경에서 ARGP (Auto-Regressive Multi-Fidelity Gaussian Processes) 를 사용하는 이동식 저수용 시간 로봇 방사선 매핑','Nuclear Technology','0029-5450','Brendon Cintas; Joel Adams; Carlos Rios; Anthony Abrahao; Dwayne McDaniel; Leonel Lagos','2026-09-22','2026-09-22','2026-09-22','연구논문','방사선·계측','This work presents an evaluation of a multi-fidelity Gaussian Process (GP) framework for fusing sparse, high-accuracy “ground-truth” radiation measurements with the dense, lower-accuracy data stream of a mobile robotic platform into a single, continuous dose-rate map with quantified uncertainty. The framework is structured in two stages: (i) an offline kernel-selection stage that fits an additive log-space discrepancy model against a fixed-location site control survey, and (ii) an online deployment stage that re-uses the offline low-fidelity GP as a Kennedy and O’Hagan auto-regressive prior over a fresh GP fit on the live robot stream. Survey data collected at a university research reactor drives the evaluation, where cross-validation across four candidate covariance kernels (Radial Basis Function, Matérn 3/2, Matérn 5/2, and Rational Quadratic) is used to assess predictive accuracy and uncertainty calibration against the control. Three results follow from treating the validation itself as an object of study. A paired bootstrap over the 21 control points shows the four kernels to be all but indistinguishable under leave-one-out cross-validation, with 34 of 36 paired differences in root mean square error containing zero. A ranking emerges only under repeated spatially-blocked cross-validation, where both Matérn kernels beat the alternatives on every trial and the ordering is close to the reverse of the leave-one-out one, quantifying how much spatial leakage that protocol admits. The model’s systematic under-prediction of high-dose zones, between 19% and 58% depending on trial and blocking radius, is shown to be substantially a property of the constant-variance likelihood rather than of data sparsity alone: substituting the detector’s Poisson counting variance removes the bias on one trial and reduces it on the others, at a cost in overall error. The selected kernel is then deployed on the robot during near real-time mapping, where the model produces continuous heatmaps of the predicted dose rate and its paired uncertainty, supporting situational awareness and conservative dose-bound planning. The primary contribution is a quantitative characterization of how the combined framework behaves on a real reactor cell, including which of its assumptions are load-bearing and which are not, and how its uncertainty output can guide conservative dose planning and active sampling.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1080/00295450.2026.2726706','https://doi.org/10.1080/00295450.2026.2726706',0,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','US','US','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.net.2026.104732','Monte Carlo simulations to determine counting efficiencies in whole-body counting measurement considering age, gender, and radionuclide biokinetics','연령, 성별 및 방사성 핵종 생체 동력학을 고려한 전신 계수 측정에서 계수 효율을 결정하기 위한 몬테카를로 시뮬레이션','Nuclear Engineering and Technology','1738-5733','MinSeok Park; Minsu Cho; Chan Hyeong Kim','2026-09-22','','2026-09','연구논문','원자로물리·해석','A large radiological release can result in internal contamination of the affected population, necessitating assessment through population monitoring methods such as whole-body counting measurements. Accurate calibration of whole-body counting systems is essential for reliable contamination assessment. However, conventional calibration methods using physical phantoms do not account for age- or gender-dependent anatomical variability. Moreover, inhaled or ingested radionuclides present radionuclide-specific biokinetics, resulting in heterogeneous activity distributions within the body that can substantially affect counting efficiency. To address these limitations, this study aims to determine time-dependent counting efficiencies for whole-body counting measurement using Monte Carlo-based calibration methods with age- and gender-specific computational human phantoms. Biodistribution data for radionuclides, including 137 Cs, 134 Cs, and 131 I, following intake are applied to the phantoms to estimate counting efficiencies. The results demonstrate that counting efficiencies are influenced by age- and gender-related anatomical differences and vary with post-intake time because of changes in biodistribution. This effect is particularly pronounced for 131 I, for which heterogeneous distribution results in significant variations in counting efficiency. By incorporating both biokinetic behavior and anatomical characteristics, the proposed approach provides more realistic estimates of counting efficiency than conventional calibration methods, thereby reducing systematic errors and supporting more accurate internal contamination assessments during radiological emergencies.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.net.2026.104732','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.net.2026.104732',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','KR','KR','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.net.2026.104714','Impact of load conditions on single event transients in power supply circuit','전원 공급 회로에서 단일 이벤트 과도 상태에 대한 부하 조건의 영향','Nuclear Engineering and Technology','1738-5733','Zhaofeng Zhen; Pan Luo; Hang Yu; Xiaohong Tan; Jun Luo; Lianchu Pan; Hantao Li','2026-09-22','','2027-02','연구논문','원자로물리·해석','Single-event transients (SETs) of the power supply circuit are investigated by conducting heavy-ion tests. The most sensitive device in the circuit whose SET response dominates the circuit functional degradation is identified. Then the impact of load currents and load types on the SET cross section of the sensitive device is analyzed. A combination of circuit characteristics tests and SPICE simulations is used to illustrate the mechanism of the influence on device SETs response under different load conditions. The research results may provide valuable assistance for power supply circuit design and device application.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.net.2026.104714','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.net.2026.104714',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','CN','CN','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.net.2026.104734','An Efficient Prediction Method of Effective Shunt Impedance for IH-Type Drift Tube Linacs','IH형 드리프트 튜브 리낙에 대한 효과적인 션트 임피던스의 효율적인 예측 방법','Nuclear Engineering and Technology','1738-5733','GuoXin Chen; ZhongShan Li; XueJun Yin; Fu Ma; MengXue Li; GuangXian Li; Liang Lu; GuoDong Shen; JianCheng Yang; JiaWen Xia','2026-09-22','','2026-09','연구논문','설계·운전·경제',': In conventional drift tube linac (DTL) design, the determination of radio-frequency (RF) parameters relies heavily on iterative three-dimensional (3D) electromagnetic (EM) simulations, which imposes several limitations, including low efficiency in evaluating multiple design schemes. To overcome this challenge, an efficient RF parameters prediction method for IH-type DTL cavities based on the Single-Cell Database Prediction (SCDP) is proposed in this paper. Multi-cell cavity models are constructed from optimized single-cell models to determine correction factors that account for the effects of undercuts and half-drift tube sections. By combining the single-cell database with beam dynamics parameters, SCDP method can efficiently predict the RF parameters of the entire cavity. Numerical validation based on the DTL1 cavity of the Ultra-rapid Cycling Accelerator Facility (UCAF) shows that the effective shunt impedance predicted by this method differs from the CST simulation result of the full-cavity model within 2%, demonstrating the numerical feasibility of the proposed method for the investigated cavity.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.net.2026.104734','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.net.2026.104734',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','CN','CN','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.net.2026.104717','Activation of the dosimetric properties of potassium chloride crystal via doping with lithium ions','리튬이온 도핑을 통한 염화칼륨 결정의 선량측정 특성 활성화','Nuclear Engineering and Technology','1738-5733','Kuanyshbek Shunkeyev; Shynar Sagimbayeva; Adelya Kenzhebayeva','2026-09-22','','2027-02','연구논문','열수력·유체','A high-temperature thermally stimulated luminescence (TSL) peak at 505 K has been identified in KCl:Li crystals. Its integral light yield ( η KCl:Li ) increases linearly with lithium ion concentration ( η KCl:Li ∼ f(С Li )), and with the dose of X-ray and gamma irradiation ( η KCl:Li ∼ f(D )). Moreover, it substantially exceeds the light yield of the dosimetric TSL peak at 495 K in the widely used TLD-100 (LiF:Mg,Ti) ( η KCl:Li >> η LiF:Mg,Ti ). The enhancement of the integral TSL light yield ( η KCl:Li ) at 505 K is attributed to an increase in the concentration of hole-type V 2 , V 3, V 4A -centers and electron-type F , F A (Li)-centers, as established by absorption, thermally activated, and luminescence spectroscopy. In KCl crystal lattice lithium ions act as catalysts of the efficient formation of both halogen centers (predominantly V 3 -centers with an absorption band peaking at 5.8 eV) and electron F -centers (2.3 eV). Upon their thermal dissociation, they serve as luminescence centers by facilitating the sequential recombination of electrons and holes released from ionized defect states.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.net.2026.104717','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.net.2026.104717',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','','','OpenAlex: country unavailable');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.nucengdes.2026.115228','Study on four-quadrant performance testing and numerical simulation of a reactor coolant pump model','원자로 냉각수 펌프 모델의 4사분면 성능 시험 및 수치 시뮬레이션에 관한 연구','Nuclear Engineering and Design','0029-5493','Haoyu Cui; Congxin Yang; Yan Wang; Xin Tan; Ye Jing; Yanlei Guo; Tianzhi Lv; Sen Zhao','2026-09-22','','2026-12','연구논문','열수력·유체','This study presents an integrated experimental–numerical investigation of the four-quadrant performance of a scaled reactor coolant pump (RCP) model. Four-quadrant experiments were conducted on a high-precision test rig to obtain the pump head, torque, and flow rate under different quadrant operating conditions. The experimental data were subsequently normalized to generate dimensionless homologous characteristic curves. Due to the limitations of the experimental setup under extreme operating conditions, numerical simulations were performed to supplement the data near the zero-flow point and under very high flow rate conditions. By combining the experimental and numerical results, a smooth and continuous full-range pump characteristic curve was obtained, which can be used in transient thermal–hydraulic analyses of reactor systems. The results improve the understanding of RCP behavior under off-design and reverse operating conditions and provide support for the development of reliable pump models for nuclear safety analyses.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nucengdes.2026.115228','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nucengdes.2026.115228',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','CN','CN','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.nucengdes.2026.115226','One dimensional model for the solid and mushy crust surrounding a terminal debris corium Pool','말단 잔해 코리움 풀을 둘러싸고 있는 단단하고 푹신한 지각에 대한 1차원 모델','Nuclear Engineering and Design','0029-5493','S. Abishek; R. David','2026-09-22','','2026-09','연구논문','기타 원자력','','','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nucengdes.2026.115226','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nucengdes.2026.115226',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','CA','CA','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.nucengdes.2026.115221','Full-core design of helical cruciform fuel for power uprate in water-cooled SMRs','수냉식 SMR에서 파워 업레이트를 위한 나선형 십자형 연료의 풀 코어 설계','Nuclear Engineering and Design','0029-5493','Youyeon Choi; Koroush Shirvan','2026-09-22','','2026-12','연구논문','열수력·유체','Helical cruciform fuel (HCF) has been proposed as an advanced fuel design for water-cooled reactors, offering enhanced safety margins through an increased heat transfer area and the potential for higher core power density compared with standard cylindrical fuel. However, comprehensive full-core neutronics design studies of HCF remain limited, particularly those exploring power uprates across different fuel forms. The unique helical geometry presents challenges for traditional two-step lattice homogenization approaches, motivating the use of direct three-dimensional full-core simulation. In this work, continuous-energy Monte Carlo simulations using the Serpent code are employed to perform a design study of three core configurations: a UO 2 cylindrical fuel core, a U-50Zr HCF core, and a UO 2 HCF core. The core design methodology is established and then applied to design U-50Zr and UO 2 HCF cores with consistent core dimensions and operating conditions. A comprehensive comparison is performed across all three configurations, evaluating key design parameters for the equilibrium cycle, such as core reactivity, critical boron letdown, power peaking factors, and reactivity coefficients. The cost assessment demonstrates that the HCF-enabled uprated core design is economically competitive with the reference design. Detailed transient safety assessments and further validation of the underlying cost assumptions remain subjects for future investigation. This study provides the first direct three-dimensional Monte Carlo core design of a water-cooled small modular reactor employing HCF for power uprate, integrated with fuel performance and economic assessments.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nucengdes.2026.115221','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nucengdes.2026.115221',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','US','US','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.nucengdes.2026.115207','Applications of artificial intelligence technology in space nuclear reactor systems','우주 원자로 시스템에 인공지능 기술 적용','Nuclear Engineering and Design','0029-5493','Shuo Liu; Kai Zhang; Dongdong Xu; Qiqian Zhai; Chenglong Wang; Lin Qi; Dalin Zhang','2026-09-22','','2026-12','연구논문','안전·사고·리스크','Space nuclear power systems have attracted increasing attention as enabling technologies for future deep-space exploration because of their high energy density, long-duration power generation capability, and independence from terrestrial environmental conditions. Artificial intelligence (AI) has shown considerable potential for addressing key challenges in space nuclear systems, including intelligent design optimization, state prediction, fault diagnosis, and autonomous operation. This review provides a systematic overview of AI applications in space nuclear power systems, with particular emphasis on current capabilities, limitations, and future development directions. The classifications, system architectures, and representative technologies of space nuclear reactors are first introduced to establish the engineering context and identify the challenges that motivate AI integration. Machine learning (ML), intelligent optimization algorithm (IOA), deep learning (DL), reduced-order model (ROM), and data assimilation (DA) are reviewed with emphasis on their applicability under the specific constraints of space nuclear systems, including limited sensing capability, stringent size, weight, and power (SWaP) requirements, radiation environments, and scarce operational data. Representative AI applications are discussed in terms of system design optimization, state prediction and digital twins (DTs), fault diagnosis, health management, and prospective autonomous operation. The current maturity of AI-enabled applications in space nuclear systems is also assessed. Existing studies rely primarily on high-fidelity simulations, ground-based demonstrations, and transferable methodologies from terrestrial nuclear engineering, while operational data from space nuclear reactors remain unavailable. Key challenges associated with onboard computational limitations, radiation-induced hardware degradation, model reliability, and verification and validation (V&V) requirements are also discussed. Future research directions toward trustworthy AI, physics-informed learning, adaptive digital twins, and intelligent autonomous operation frameworks are then summarized. This review provides a reference for understanding the current status and future potential of AI technologies in space nuclear power systems.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nucengdes.2026.115207','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nucengdes.2026.115207',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','CN','CN','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112839','Taxonomies of levels of automation for small modular reactors based on operational conditions','작동 조건에 따른 소형모듈원자로(SMR)의 자동화 수준 분류 체계','Annals of Nuclear Energy','0306-4549','Huijeong Kim; Jonghyun Kim','2026-09-22','','2027-02','연구논문','안전·사고·리스크','Many fields, including the nuclear energy industry, commonly use a taxonomy of levels of automation (LOAs) as a reference for function allocation, a process conducted to determine whether functions are assigned to humans or automation. An emerging issue is that existing LOA taxonomies have limitations when applied to small modular reactors (SMRs), as SMRs tend to adopt higher levels of automation and expanded passive safety systems than large nuclear power plants. This paper proposes detailed LOA taxonomies that reflect the operational characteristics of SMRs under different operational conditions—normal, abnormal, and emergency conditions—with specific consideration of operator support systems as a major automation domain. For normal operations, the LOA taxonomies are divided into system automation and operation automation based on whether control systems are utilized. For abnormal operations, in which operators’ cognitive functions are emphasized, the taxonomy is classified based on data acquisition, diagnosis, and abnormal operating procedure implementation, with various roles of automation as an operator support system. For emergency conditions, the taxonomy integrates the concepts of passive safety systems and automation. The proposed LOA taxonomies provide a foundation for defining human–automation roles in greater detail from the early stage of SMR design, as well as support designers in considering different types and levels of operator support systems.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112839','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112839',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','KR','KR','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112870','Successive criteria analysis approach for mitigating Deterministic hydrogen risk in severe accidents','심각한 사고에서 결정적 수소 위험 완화를 위한 연속적인 기준 분석 접근법','Annals of Nuclear Energy','0306-4549','Saied Soltanmohammadi; Ataollah Rabiee; Kamal Hadad; MohammadReza Nematollahi','2026-09-22','','2026-09','연구논문','안전·사고·리스크','','','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112870','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112870',0,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','IR','IR','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112859','On Physics-Based Loss Scaling for MF-PINNs applied to the neutron diffusion equation','중성자 확산 방정식에 적용된 MF-PINN에 대한 물리 기반 손실 스케일링','Annals of Nuclear Energy','0306-4549','Minh Hieu Do; François Madiot; Karim Ammar; Nicolas Gérard Castaing','2026-09-22','','2027-02','연구논문','원자로물리·해석','','','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112859','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112859',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','','','OpenAlex: country unavailable');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112867','Numerical investigation of top-flooding debris bed quenching FLOAT experiments','상부 침수 잔해층 담금질 플로트 실험에 대한 수치 조사','Annals of Nuclear Energy','0306-4549','Mitja Uršič; Tim Kelhar; Anej Kukovič; Matjaž Leskovar; Leon Cizelj; Markus Petroff; Rudi Kulenovic; Jörg Starflinger; Renaud Meignen; Janez Kokalj','2026-09-22','','2027-02','연구논문','열수력·유체','Safety relevant questions during severe accidents in a nuclear power plant may concern the ex-vessel debris bed coolability in combination with molten corium concrete interaction. The behaviour within the debris bed is expected to be multidimensional, as the bed may be irregular in geometry, composed of heterogeneous particles, exhibit spatially varying porosity, be flooded from different directions, partially re-melt due to local or global dry out, and be affected by non-condensable gases. Fundamental investigation of such conditions requires consideration of multidimensional behaviour. It can be supported by top-flooding experiments and simulations with validated computer codes. This paper evaluates the suitability of an axisymmetric description for analysing multidimensional behaviour during debris bed quenching under top-flooding conditions. Analyses of recent FLOAT experiments (IKE, Germany) show that the multidimensionality of the quenching process is governed by the location of the penetrating water. A detailed numerical investigation performed with the MC3D code (ASNR, France) provides additional valuable insights supporting the interpretation of the experimental results. The axisymmetric modelling shows good qualitative agreement with the measurements. An improved quantitative description is possible with non-axisymmetric modelling. Sensitivity to air flow, initial temperature, porosity, and friction modelling is observed.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112867','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112867',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','SI;DE;FR','SI','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112861','Joint study on anomaly detection and remaining useful life prediction of ex-core neutron detector in nuclear power plant under interference factors','간섭 요인 하에서 원자력 발전소에서 전핵 중성자 검출기의 이상 검출 및 잔류 유용 수명 예측에 관한 공동 연구','Annals of Nuclear Energy','0306-4549','Yong Xu; Yunze Cai; Lin Song; Ruzhen Yang; Huixin Wu; Xianxuan Huang','2026-09-22','','2027-02','연구논문','원자로물리·해석','The remaining useful life (RUL) prediction of the ex-core neutron detector is needed to provide helpful references for reactor operation, enable equipment condition monitoring, and enhance condition-based maintenance (CBM) in nuclear power plant (NPP). Most equipment RUL prediction systems fail to integrate anomaly detection methods to determine the onset time of anomalies and neglect the interference factors generating the outliers. We demonstrate the effectiveness of combining the virtual redundancy anomaly detection model with the RUL prediction model in overcoming these issues, based on operational data from a set of real-world intermediate range detectors (IRDs). We also propose an outlier detection method to remove the outliers generated by interference factors. Moreover, a RUL prediction model and a deterioration trend tracking prediction model have been developed.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112861','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112861',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','CN','CN','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112871','Iterative bidirectional thermo-mechanical coupling framework for reactor lower head failure analysis under severe accident conditions','심각한 사고 조건에서 원자로 하부 헤드 고장 분석을 위한 반복 양방향 열-기계 커플링 프레임워크','Annals of Nuclear Energy','0306-4549','Zibin Huang; Runze Zhai; Bin Zhang; Shaowei Tang; Hao Yang; Fan Miao; Jianqiang Shan','2026-09-22','','2027-02','연구논문','안전·사고·리스크','','','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112871','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112871',0,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','CN','CN','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112823','A new stochastic implicit depletion scheme with combined nuclide-flux relaxation and a review of computational efficiency and accuracy for stochastic implicit depletion schemes','핵종 유동 이완을 결합한 새로운 확률적 암시적 연소 계획 및 확률적 암시적 연소 계획에 대한 계산 효율성 및 정확성 검토','Annals of Nuclear Energy','0306-4549','Jonathon Faulkner; Bojan Petrovic','2026-09-22','','2027-02','리뷰·관점','원자로물리·해석','Depletion calculations using the Monte-Carlo method for neutron transport commonly suffer from numerical instabilities due to a phenomenon known as xenon-power oscillations. Such oscillations are physical in nature and are regularly observed in real nuclear reactors; but the feedback between spatial xenon concentration and the spatial power distribution in most Monte-Carlo depletion simulations is nonphysical and creates stability issues when depletion is modeled in optically thick problems such as full-length fuel assemblies or full cores. The equilibrium xenon approach or stochastic implicit (SI) depletion schemes are commonly used to ameliorate these issues as resolving xenon-power oscillations by simply increasing the number of simulated histories is usually an intractable approach. This study extends a previously proposed constant relaxation predictor–corrector SI approach (SI-CE/LI) by considering a new scheme with constant relaxation of both the spatial nuclide density and flux (or power) distributions. The new scheme is called the SI-CE/LI-Flux-Nuclide (SI-CE/LI-FN) scheme. Combined relaxation of both the spatial nuclide and flux distributions has a synergistic effect and the proposed methodology is able to obtain stable solutions to a 700 cm tall Fluoride-salt High temperature Reactor fuel assembly problem. With properly chosen relaxation factors, performance is equal, or better, than that of single-field relaxation approaches used in SI depletion schemes. Moreover, SI approaches offer a new way to split computational effort in already computationally intense depletion calculations; therefore, the apportioning of computational effort when using SI schemes is further investigated and practical guidance is given for users of SI depletion schemes.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112823','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112823',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','US','US','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106613','Source-term modeling of radioactive krypton and xenon in activated carbon delay beds for molten salt reactor off-gas systems','용융 염 원자로 오프 가스 시스템용 활성탄 지연층에서 방사성 크립톤 및 크세논의 소스 기간 모델링','Progress in Nuclear Energy','0149-1970','Yunfei Zhang; Guifeng Zhu; Xuechao Zhao; Rui Yan; Yang Zou','2026-09-21','','2027-01','연구논문','핵연료·재료','Radioactive krypton (Kr) and xenon (Xe) continuously released from liquid-fueled molten salt reactors are transported to the cover-gas and off-gas treatment systems, where activated carbon delay beds play an important role in source-term control. This study develops a transient modeling framework for Kr/Xe transport, retention, decay, and activity evolution in activated carbon delay beds. The one-dimensional model couples axial convection, axial dispersion, linear driving force adsorption, Henry-equilibrium adsorption, and radioactive decay. The formulation is extended to multi-nuclide decay chains, allowing gaseous daughters to continue transport and adsorption while treating non-gaseous daughters as locally deposited products. Reactor-condition-dependent inlet source terms are provided through one-way coupling with MACT-based multi-node source-term calculations, including upstream holdup and pre-bed decay. The numerical implementation is verified against an analytical solution under the local-equilibrium limit and using the Method of Manufactured Solutions (MMS) for finite-rate Henry–LDF coupling, while the adsorption model is evaluated against dilute Kr fixed-bed breakthrough data. A reference case inspired by the Molten Salt Reactor Experiment (MSRE) is then analyzed to examine isotope-dependent penetration, parent-decay feeding, phase-resolved activity distribution, nuclide-wise activity contributions, and shutdown residual activity. The results show strong isotope-dependent attenuation: Kr-85m falls below 10 −3 of its inlet concentration at x/L ≈ 0.224, whereas Kr-85 penetrates the full bed and Xe-131m remains measurable at the outlet. Parent decay can noticeably enhance daughter concentrations; for the Kr-85m→Kr-85 chain, in-bed feeding increases the maximum normalized Kr-85 concentration to approximately 2.3. At 180 d, the adsorbed and deposited inventories account for approximately 99.6% of the bed-integrated activity, while the gas phase accounts for only 0.38%. Following shutdown, continued helium purge reduces the gas-phase inventory to 10.8% of its shutdown value at 0.83 d, whereas the total residual activity decreases more slowly, reaching 0.7% of its shutdown value at 30 d.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106613','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106613',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','CN','CN','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106619','Research on water radiolysis models and their behavior in high flux research reactors','고유속 연구용 원자로에서의 수질방사선분해모형과 그 거동에 관한 연구','Progress in Nuclear Energy','0149-1970','Yi Huang; Wei Peng; Xinhe Qu; Jian Li; Heng Xie','2026-09-21','','2027-01','연구논문','열수력·유체','A water radiolysis model is developed for high flux research reactors, with application to the Tsinghua High Flux Reactor (THFR). Refined G-value calculations (gamma and neutron) and supplemented reactions for alkaline conditions are validated against literature experiments, showing good agreement. Parametric studies reveal that molecular product concentrations scale with the square root of the dose rate due to the bimolecular nature of elementary reactions, and decrease with temperature, with a progressively slowing rate of decrease, reflecting different temperature dependencies of the competing production and consumption kinetics. THFR simulations show steady state reached within ∼1 s in most regions. Notably, despite the dose rate being two orders of magnitude lower than that in the core, upper and lower plena exhibit significant importance on the coolant radiolysis due to the square root scaling and long residence times.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106619','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106619',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','CN','CN','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106611','First-principles insights into step-site catalysis on Pt–Pd alloys for passive autocatalytic recombiners under oxygen-rich conditions','산소가 풍부한 조건 하에서 수동적 자가촉매 재조합체를 위한 Pt–Pd 합금에 대한 계단 부위 촉매에 대한 첫 번째 원칙 통찰력','Progress in Nuclear Energy','0149-1970','Jushang Zhang; Youcai Feng; Lingzhe Meng; Yifan Yang; Yunhe Zhao; Ming Ding; Zehua Guo','2026-09-21','','2027-01','연구논문','안전·사고·리스크','Passive autocatalytic recombiners are essential safety devices for mitigating hydrogen hazards in nuclear power plant containments. Their performance is determined by the catalytic activity of the Pt–Pd alloy surfaces employed. In practice, these surfaces are highly irregular and rich in step defects. The atomic-scale mechanisms of hydrogen recombination at these step sites under oxygen-rich conditions remain poorly understood. In this work, we used first-principles calculations to systematically investigate the step-site reactivity of Pt 3 Pd (111) surfaces for catalytic hydrogen recombination. We reveal a coverage-dependent cooperative oxygen binding mechanism between up-step and down-step sites. Pre-adsorbed up-step oxygen atoms stabilize the otherwise metastable down-step site by nearly 1 eV through electronic effects, reducing the OH* formation barrier from 1.167 eV to 0.695 eV. This translates into a rate enhancement of approximately four orders of magnitude at 500 K. Electronic structure analyses, including Bader charge and projected density of states, demonstrate that this cooperative effect originates from cumulative depletion of d-band electrons from step-edge Pd and Pt atoms by pre-adsorbed oxygen, which weakens the vertical metal–oxygen bond. Kinetic analysis reveals three competing OH* consumption pathways with distinct site-dependent rates. At 500 K, the ordering follows up-step > free > up-step, with rate ratios of approximately 2:1:0.002. These results establish a mechanistic framework for understanding step-mediated catalysis on alloy surfaces under oxygen-rich conditions and provide atomic-scale guidance for the design of high-performance PAR catalysts for nuclear safety applications.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106611','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106611',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','CN','CN','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106624','Development and verification of the COME coupling platform for full-core pin-by-pin steady-state and transient analysis of the OECD/NEA PWR MOX/UO2 core transient benchmark','OECD/NEA PWR MOX/UO2 코어 과도 벤치마크의 풀 코어 핀별 정상 상태 및 과도 분석을 위한 COM 커플링 플랫폼 개발 및 검증','Progress in Nuclear Energy','0149-1970','Yangyi Zhang; Ye Chen; Yunlin Xu; Xiafeng Zhou','2026-09-21','','2027-01','연구논문','열수력·유체','High-resolution full-core neutronics/thermal-hydraulics coupling remains computationally demanding because of the large number of spatial and angular degrees of freedom, strong nonlinear feedback, and repeated physics evaluations required in steady-state and transient calculations. In this work, the COupling Multiphysics Environment (COME) is extended to full-core pin-by-pin analysis of the OECD/NEA PWR MOX/UO 2 core transient benchmark. The coupled model combines the three-dimensional multigroup discrete-ordinates transport solver comeSn with the pin-resolved thermal-hydraulics and fuel heat-conduction solver comeSC. Response-based residual construction is used to preserve the standalone solver implementations. A global Picard–Jacobian-free Newton–Krylov (JFNK) strategy is applied to the neutronics-only and coupled critical-boron-concentration calculations, whereas a nested Picard/JFNK strategy is used for the rod-ejection transient. All COME calculations directly use the benchmark-provided eight-group pin-cell-homogenized cross-section library, thereby avoiding variability introduced by independent cross-section generation. For Part 1, author-generated multigroup OpenMC calculations using the same library and geometry provide a controlled same-library verification reference. The COME–OpenMC k eff differences are 18 pcm for the all-rods-out case and 39 pcm for the all-rods-in case. The corresponding signed pin-power differences range from −2.06% to +1.41% and from −2.54% to +3.35%, respectively. Parts 2–4 are assessed against the spread of published benchmark solutions because no independent same-library three-dimensional coupled reference is available. COME predicts critical boron concentrations of 1678 ppm at hot full power and 1336 ppm at hot zero power, an effective delayed neutron fraction of 577 pcm, and rod-ejection transient metrics of 0.229 s for peak time, 441% for peak power, 1.24 dollars of peak reactivity, and 40.3%-s power integral. These results demonstrate the implementation and assessment of a three-dimensional pin-resolved transport/thermal-hydraulics workflow within COME and the problem-dependent organization of its nonlinear coupling strategies. The reported results provide a reproducible deterministic transport-based comparison dataset under a fully specified pin-cell cross-section specification and may support future code-to-code assessment and method development.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106624','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106624',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','CN;US','CN','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1080/00295450.2026.2697690','Foreword: Special Issue Featuring Papers from the 2024 Advances in Thermal Hydraulics Conference (ATH 2024)','서문: 2024 Advances in Thermal Hydraulics Conference (ATH 2024) 의 논문이 수록된 특별호','Nuclear Technology','0029-5450','Dillon Shaver; Igor Bolotnov; Ferry Roelofs; Victor Petrov; Subash Sharma','2026-09-21','2026-09-21','2026-09-21','연구논문','열수력·유체','The organizers of the 2024 Advances in Thermal Hydraulics Conference (ATH 2024) welcome our readers and colleagues to this special issue of Nuclear Technology. Herein we present a selection of peer...','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1080/00295450.2026.2697690','https://doi.org/10.1080/00295450.2026.2697690',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','US;DE;EE;CH','US','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1080/00295450.2026.2726711','Development and Optimization of a Photodiode-Based Wide-Range Gamma Dose Rate Monitor for Nuclear Applications','원자력 응용 분야를 위한 포토다이오드 기반 광범위 감마선량률 모니터 개발 및 최적화','Nuclear Technology','0029-5450','Sanjay Singh; Riya Dey; Niraj Chaddha; Liji S; S. Anand; Suresh Kumar MK','2026-09-21','2026-09-21','2026-09-21','연구논문','원자로물리·해석','Accurate gamma dose rate measurement is essential for radiation protection, environmental monitoring, and process control in nuclear facilities operating under high-radiation conditions. One of the key requirements of a measurement system is operational flexibility, portability, and the capability to perform measurements over a wide range. In response to these requirements, this study presents the development and optimization of a compact photodiode-based gamma dose rate monitoring system with a wide dynamic range for high dose-rate applications. The work primarily focused on improving the detector energy response through the design and optimization of an energy-compensation filter using Monte Carlo simulations performed with the FLUKA radiation transport code. The uncompensated detector exhibited significant over-response at lower photon energies due to the energy dependence of photon interactions within the detector material. To mitigate this effect, a graded compensation filter comprising copper and stainless-steel layers was designed and optimized. Experimental characterization was carried out using ISO 4037 narrow-spectrum X-ray beam qualities of 80–250 kV, along with 137Cs and 60Co gamma-ray sources. The optimized detector configuration demonstrated a nearly energy-independent response over the photon-energy range from 100 keV to 1.3 MeV. Experimental measurements showed good agreement with simulation predictions, validating the proposed compensation methodology. Dose rate linearity measurements performed up to 10 000 R/h using a 60Co source demonstrated linear detector response with deviations within ±15% of reference values. The developed system offers a compact, lightweight, and low-power alternative to conventional ionization chamber-based monitors for wide-range gamma dose-rate measurements in nuclear fuel cycle facilities.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1080/00295450.2026.2726711','https://doi.org/10.1080/00295450.2026.2726711',0,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','IN','IN','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.net.2026.104715','A flexible silicone elastomer/copper oxide nanocomposite for current-mode X-ray dosimetry: Synthesis, characterization, and performance evaluation','전류 모드 X선 선량 측정을 위한 유연한 실리콘 엘라스토머/구리 산화물 나노 복합체: 합성, 특성 분석 및 성능 평가','Nuclear Engineering and Technology','1738-5733','Farideh Keyvani; Leila Gholamzadeh; Shahryar Malekie; Sedigheh Kashian','2026-09-21','','2027-02','연구논문','핵연료·재료','Flexible, polymer-based nanocomposite dosimeters have attracted growing interest for radiation monitoring in diagnostic radiology because of their mechanical conformability and potential for low-cost, large-area fabrication. While silicone-based detectors loaded with heavier metal oxides have previously been explored, the dosimetric behavior of room-temperature-vulcanizing (RTV-2) silicone elastomer reinforced with copper oxide nanoparticles — which differs from these systems in both filler chemistry and cure mechanism — has not been systematically characterized. In this work, a flexible silicone/copper oxide nanocomposite was fabricated and evaluated, for the first time, as a current-mode X-ray dosimeter. Copper oxide nanoparticles were synthesized by a simple precipitation method and incorporated into the silicone matrix at weight fractions ranging from 0 to 36.4 wt percent. Structural and chemical characterization confirmed the formation of phase-pure, monoclinic copper oxide nanoparticles with an average crystallite size of 27–29 nm, dispersed within the amorphous silicone network as submicron aggregates (mean Feret diameter 253 ± 86 nm within the composite, from 121 measured particles). Under 60 kV diagnostic X-ray irradiation, the nanocomposite exhibited a stable, nearly linear photocurrent response over a dose-rate range of 2.8–12 mGy/min. Among the compositions evaluated, the sample containing approximately 27.6 wt percent copper oxide showed the best overall dosimetric performance, with a sensitivity of 0.102 ± 0.014 nA/(mGy/min) and a noise-limited detection threshold of 0.25 mGy/min; given that the composition-dependent variation in photocurrent (∼11%) is comparable to the measurement uncertainty, this composition is best regarded as a favorable rather than a definitively optimized formulation. The enhanced sensitivity relative to the unfilled matrix is attributed primarily to the higher photon-interaction probability associated with the increased effective atomic number introduced by the copper oxide phase. The devices showed good short-term repeatability, with a relative standard deviation of 1.01% over repeated irradiation cycles, and a signal-to-noise ratio exceeding 90 at an applied bias of 400 V. These results indicate that silicone/copper oxide nanocomposites constitute a cost-effective, mechanically flexible platform for low-energy X-ray dosimetry, with performance comparable to other polymer–metal oxide systems reported in the literature.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.net.2026.104715','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.net.2026.104715',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','IR','IR','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.nucengdes.2026.115229','Flow-based temperature regulation during unprotected fuel salt overcooling transient in molten salt fast reactors','용융 염 고속 원자로에서 비보호 연료 염 과냉각 과도 동안 유량 기반 온도 조절','Nuclear Engineering and Design','0029-5493','Thanh Hung Nguyen; Yitung Chen','2026-09-21','','2026-12','연구논문','열수력·유체','Molten Salt Fast Reactors (MSFRs) present unique safety and control challenges due to the absence of conventional mechanical reactivity control mechanisms and the circulation of fuel salt. In particular, unprotected fuel salt overcooling (UFSOC) transient can lead to power excursion and increased temperature gradients within the core. In this study, steady state and transient computational fluid dynamics (CFD) simulations were performed to investigate MSFR behavior. A UFSOC transient was analyzed, and a flow-based control strategy using a proportional–integral–derivative (PID) controller was implemented to regulate the primary pump speed based on deviations in the core inlet temperature. Steady state results reveal the formation of a recirculation zone near the blanket region, leading to localized temperature peaks exceeding the core outlet temperature. During the UFSOC transient, enhanced heat extraction in the intermediate heat exchanger induces a rapid power increase while maintaining a relatively stable average core temperature, but significantly increases temperature gradients. The proposed control strategy effectively limits excessive cooling, restores the core inlet temperature toward nominal conditions, and reduces thermal gradients. Increased flow enhances mixing and suppresses localized hot spots, resulting in a more uniform temperature field. These results demonstrate that the flow-based control can significantly enhance the stability and safety of MSFRs under UFSOC transient conditions, supporting its application in future MSFR designs.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nucengdes.2026.115229','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nucengdes.2026.115229',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','VN;US','US','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.nucengdes.2026.115213','Assessment of RANS models for flow mixing in large enclosures using high-resolution experiments and LES','고해상도 실험 및 LES를 사용하여 대형 인클로저에서 흐름 혼합을 위한 RANS 모델 평가','Nuclear Engineering and Design','0029-5493','Jiaxin Mao; Victor Coppo Leite; Victor Petrov; Annalisa Manera','2026-09-21','','2026-12','연구논문','열수력·유체','Accurate prediction of turbulent mixing in large enclosures is essential for the safety analysis of High-Temperature Gas-cooled Reactors (HTGRs), particularly during extended Loss of Forced Circulation (LOFC) accident scenarios. Despite its industrial relevance, this phenomenon remains challenging to model due to complex three-dimensional flow interactions and multi-scale effects involved, including jet impingement, shear-layer development, and recirculation, which are characteristic flow mechanisms in large plena. In this work, Reynolds-Averaged Navier–Stokes (RANS) turbulence models are systematically evaluated against a high-quality validation database composed of high-resolution experiments and Large Eddy Simulation (LES). The study focuses on an isothermal single-jet injection case in the 1/12-scale Michigan Multi-jet Gas-mixture Dome (MiGaDome) facility, which is representative of upper plenum mixing in HTGRs. Four commonly used turbulence models—the Standard 𝑘 − ɛ Low-Re, Realizable 𝑘 − ɛ , Reynolds Stress Transport, and 𝑘 − 𝜔 SST models—are assessed by comparing first- and second-order turbulence statistics on multiple planes within the enclosure. Quantitative error metrics are employed to evaluate model performance relative to experimental and LES data. Results show that the Standard 𝑘 − ɛ Low-Re and Realizable 𝑘 − ɛ models provide the most accurate predictions of mean velocity fields, while all RANS models significantly underpredict turbulence intensity and second-order statistics. These findings highlight both the strengths and limitations of RANS approaches for confined jet mixing and provide guidance for their application in reactor safety analyses, as well as a foundation for future development of reduced-fidelity models informed by high-resolution data.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nucengdes.2026.115213','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nucengdes.2026.115213',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','US;CH','US','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112865','Numerical investigation on characteristics of onset of flow instability (OFI) and flow and heat transfer in petal-shape fuel assembly','꽃잎 모양 연료 어셈블리에서 유동 불안정성 (OFI) 및 유동 및 열전달의 시작 특성에 대한 수치 조사','Annals of Nuclear Energy','0306-4549','Lipeng Du; Pengyue Zhang; Xiang Chen; Wenchao Zhang; Jianchuang Sun; Weihua Cai','2026-09-21','','2027-02','연구논문','열수력·유체','Onset of Flow Instability (OFI) marks the threshold at which a two-phase flow system transitions from stable to unstable operation. Particularly in narrow flow channels, the premature occurrence of this point will significantly elevate risks of nuclear reactors. Apply the models of Eulerian two-phase flow and the RPI wall boiling, the subcooled flow boiling in the petal-shaped fuel assembly is simulated. This study reveals the mechanism of key operating parameters affect the OFI point, and explains the flow field patterns and heat transfer characteristics near the OFI cases. The result show that when the heating power increases, the critical mass flow rate at the OFI increases, while the effect of inlet subcooling is opposite: the higher the subcooling, the lower the critical mass flow rate. Among all parameters, operating pressure has the most significant influence. An increase in operating pressure reduces the critical mass flow rate at the OFI, but raises the critical void fraction required for instability. Before the OFI, bubbles mainly adhere near the heated wall. After the OFI, the vortex flow rapidly detaches bubbles from the wall and transports them toward the channel center, while bubble accumulation appears in the vortex core region. After the OFI, large-scale vortex structures are generated in the channel, especially in the rear half. These vortices are driven by strong pressure differences and significantly enhance transverse mixing between subchannels, increasing the local cross flow velocity by 200%-300%. The intense mixing effectively promotes heat transfer from the wall to the channel center, resulting in a more uniform temperature distribution across the cross section in the rear half of the channel after OFI, and the wall temperature becomes lower than that under higher flow rate conditions, while the overall convective heat transfer coefficient is improved.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112865','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.anucene.2026.112865',1,'2026-09-27','2026-09-28','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','CN','CN','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
INSERT INTO "papers" VALUES('10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106636','Experimental and numerical investigation of LBE flow and heat transfer in a grid-spaced hexagonal 7-rod bundle with cosine axial power distribution','코사인 축 방향 전력 분포가 있는 격자 간격의 육각형 7-로드 번들에서의 LBE 흐름 및 열전달에 대한 실험적 및 수치적 조사','Progress in Nuclear Energy','0149-1970','Zi''ang Li; Lanfei Yuan; Chenglong Wang; Dalin Zhang; Pengrui Qiao; Suizheng Qiu; Hanyang Gu','2026-10-04','','2027-01','연구논문','열수력·유체','To support thermal-hydraulic design and safety assessment of lead-cooled fast reactor fuel assemblies, this study investigates the flow and heat transfer characteristics of lead-bismuth eutectic (LBE) in a grid-spaced hexagonal 7-rod bundle under a cosine axial power distribution. Experiments were performed over a Reynolds number range of 2.4 × 10 4 to 5.5 × 10 4 using heater rods with an axial cosine power profile. The measured Nusselt numbers are significantly lower than those predicted by classical correlations developed for uniform heating. The validated CFD analysis suggests that this discrepancy is mainly associated with the continuous restructuring of the thermal boundary layer under cosine heat flux and the inherent confinement effects of the bundle geometry. For the present low-blockage, vane-free grid, CFD predicts a flow–thermal decoupling behavior, characterized by pronounced flow disturbances accompanied by comparatively limited local heat-transfer enhancement. Using the experimental measurements together with the validated CFD results, the heat-transfer phase lag caused by the thermal entrance effect was quantified, and a configuration-specific empirical correlation for the axial Nu distribution under cosine heating was developed for the present bundle configuration and investigated operating range. Furthermore, the validated CFD results showed that, driven by fluid thermal inertia, the peak cladding temperature was located at approximately z / L = 0.70 within the heated section, downstream of the power peak at z / L = 0.50, and remained insensitive to variations in heat load within the investigated range. These findings offer significant insights into boundary layer restructuring in low-Pr fluids and provide a valuable reference for the thermal-hydraulic design of LFR fuel assemblies.','OpenAlex','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106636','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.pnucene.2026.106636',1,'2026-10-04','2026-10-05','active','Crossref + OpenAlex','CN','CN','OpenAlex authorship institutions');
